1 OCCU farmer of about 1500 acresin Opst Settlement in Franklin County, Pa
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR# [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_descendants/?action=list&MyPrimary_Seqn=475217&MyLineageCount=1 A014551]
2 CONT
2 CONT Private 2nd Class Jacob Michael Brinker Sr.
2 CONT 5th Company, 6th Battalion, Northampton County Militia (Revolutionary War)
2 CONT Born in Switzerland on November 9, 1727 as Jacob Brungger, Jacob immigrated to Pennsylvania as a child in 1735, where his family's name became Brinker. He grew up clearing land, farming, and eventually building a log flour mill in
2 CONC  Sciota, PA, which became known as Brinker's Mill.
2 CONT During the Revolutionary War, Jacob served his country not just on the field with the Northampton County Militia, but also through his livelihood. In 1779, under orders from General George Washington, Brinker’s Mill served as a cr
2 CONC ucial storehouse and advance post for Maj. Gen. John Sullivan's expedition, supplying and feeding thousands of American troops.
2 CONT Following the war, Jacob and his wife Susanna moved to Westmoreland County, purchasing an 800-acre tract of land in present day Penn Township. Deeply tied to his faith and community, Jacob became a founder of the Denmark Manor Ref
2 CONC ormed Church. His legacy also includes donating a portion of his land, alongside Conrad Knappenberger, to establish the Denmark Manor Church, a beautiful piece of local history that still stands today.
2 CONT Jacob passed away on October 30, 1798. Though his original gravestone at Denmark Manor is now gone or illegible, his immense contributions to the founding of our nation and our local community are firmly cemented in history.
2 CONT We thank Private Jacob Michael Brinker Sr. for his service and sacrifice! 
2 CONT                               - Penn Township Historical Society Westmoreland Co.
