1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mülheim an der Ruhr, also called "City on the River", is a city in North Rhine-Westphalia in Germany. It is located in the Ruhr Area between Duisburg, Essen, Oberhausen and [Ratingen]. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki
2 CONC /M%C3%BClheim Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT * name notes: French/German Original spellings of DeHaven are Ten heuven (Dutch), in den Hoofen, Evert in den Hooffen (Germany), Evertt in den Hoven (Germany), In deHoffee and IndenHove (early Pennsylvania Deeds), Inn den Hoff (Na
2 CONC turalization record) to Dehaven by about 1750. (thought to be the original French spelling).
2 CONT
2 CONT * parents: Theis In Den Hoffen & Anna Gerdraut
2 CONT
2 CONT * From the History of Skippack & Vicinity, James Y Heckler:
2 CONT
2 CONT The first DeHavens in America were Evert (Edward) ten Heuven (ImHoff, Endehave, Indehoffen, In den Hoffen, Dehaven) his wife and four children who came from Mulheim on the Ruhr, Germany in 1698. They settled near Philadelphia, Pen
2 CONC nsylvania. Edward In den Hoffen and wife Elizabeth (Shipbower) were still living March 4, 1728 when they deed their 100-acre tract to Peter Indehoffen. The earliest residence recorded was in old Germantown, 1698. Evert is said to 
2 CONC have been a Ruling Elder at the Skippack German Reformed Church in 1706 but a deed shows that he bought 100 acres of land in Whitpain Township,1706, the land being bounded by ....."said Edward Endehaven's other Land." In 1710 the 
2 CONC names of Evert Ten Hueven, his wife and children appear in the old Bensalem history of the Presbyterian Church. Three of his children were married there in 1711/1712: Harmon, Peter, and Annaken.  Evert was then the Senior Elder of
2 CONC  the early church at Wytmess (Whitemarsh), a township southeast of Whitpain and not far from the present boundaries of Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=declub&id=I2995 Rootsweb database]
2 CONT
2 CONT ======== 
2 CONT The DeHaven Family in America, Phil and Montgomery Cos. PA
2 CONT
2 CONT The first DeHavens in America were Evert (Edward0 ten Heuven, (Im Hoff, Edehave, Indehoffen, In den Hoffen, DeHaven, his wife four children who caem from MYlheim on the RYhr, Germany in 1698. They settled near Philadephia, Pennsyl
2 CONC vania. Edward In de Hoffen wife Elizabeth (Shipbower) were still living Mary 4, 1728 when they deeded their 100 acre tract to Peter Indefoffen. The earliest residence recorded was in Old Germantown, 1698. Evert is said to have bee
2 CONC n Ruling Elder at the Sippack German Reformed Church in 1706 but a deed shows that he bouth 100 acres of land in Whitpain Township, 1706, the land being bounded by......." said Edward Endehaven's other land." In 1710 the names of 
2 CONC Evert ten Heuven, his wife and children appear in the Bensalem history of the Presbyterian Church. Three of his children were married there in 1711-12: Hermon , Peter and Annken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evert was the Senior Elder of the early church at Wytmess (Whitemarsh), a township southeast of Whitpain and not far from the present Boundaries of Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The apparent incongruities in the early history of Evert (Edward) Heuven (Endeheuvan) disappear as on studies the early land transfers in which various spellings of the fmaily name are brought together as indentical, and as one re
2 CONC ads the church history which says that some of the same persons instrumental in organizing the corresponding Reformed Churches. The variation in spelling the name. It is quite evident that Evert Indenhoffen had some contact with F
2 CONC rancis Pastorius in early settlement Skippack river and that he lived in the southeastern of the tract along the Township which was contigous with Whitemarsh. Naturally he would have been one of the nearby Wytmess congregartion an
2 CONC d indubitably assisted in "ten Heuvens" were not mentioned by Van Vlecq as being among those who were "the nucleus of rev. Boehm's German Reformed Congregation at Wytmess on Dec 26,1726" although various lands of Peter Indehave we
2 CONC re adjacent to the land upon which Boehm's Church was later built at Blue Bell, Whitpain Township.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Evert Ten Heuven was born 1657 in Mulheim, Ruhr, Germany,, and died 1728 in Skippack Twp, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  (Skippack Township is a township in Montgomery County, Pennsylvania   [http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?t
2 CONC itle=Special%3ASearch&profile=default&search=Skippack+Twp%2C+++Pennsylvania&fulltext=Search  Wikipedia] ).  He married Elizabeth Shipbower 21 May 1675 in Germany. She was born Abt 1660 in Germany, and died Aft 1728 in , , Pennsylv
2 CONC ania.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Evert Ten Heuven and Elizabeth Shipbower are:
2 CONT  *  2   i. Anna Gerdruth was born 1676 in Germany, and died in Germany. She married Stoffel Stahle 1702 in Germany.  
2 CONT *   3   ii. Willm Dehaven was christened 29 Nov 1680 in Mylheim And Der Ryhr, Germany, died in Germany. He married Treyngen Auff Den Bollemberg 1694 in Germany.  
2 CONT * + 4   iii. Gerhard (Garret) Dehaven was born in Menden, Westphalia German, was christened 12 May 1681 in Reformaed Church, Mylheim An Der Ryhr, Germany, and died in Feb 1746. 
2 CONT * + 5   iv. Harmon Dehaven was christened 1682 in Germany, died 1752 in New Providence, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT * + 6   v. Peter Dehaven was born 1686 in Mylheim On The Ryhr, Germany, and died 23 May 1768 in Blue Bell, Whitpain Township, Montgomery (Then Philadelphia) County, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT * + 7   vi. Annaken Dehaven was born in Mylheim An Der Ryhr, Germany, was christened 7 Jan 1691 in Mylheim An Der Ryhr, Germany, and died Aft 1752. 
2 CONT *   8   vii. Welhelm Dehaven was christened 26 Mar 1695, died infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=REG&db=monkeys&id=I15557 Janet Ariciu family Bush]
2 CONT
2 CONT =====================
2 CONT
2 CONT Per the [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=REG&db=declub&id=I7816 DeHAVEN & Relations DeHAVEN & Relations] cited above  he is   the son of   Wilhelm In Den Hoven &  Grete Aufon Kampee
2 CONT
2 CONT In this database their family is given as:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=declub&id=I7816 Wilhelm In Den Hoven]  was born 1620 in , Germany. He married Grete Aufon Kampee 1648 in , Germany. She was born 1625 in , Germany, and died 1652 in , G
2 CONC ermany.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Wilhelm In Den Hoven and Grete Aufon Kampee are:
2 CONT * + 2   i. [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=declub&id=I2995 Evert In den Hoffen DeHAVEN ]  was born 21 May 1650 in Mulheim, Westphalia, Germany, was christened 1650 in Mulheim a d Ruhr, Germany, and died 
2 CONC AFT 4 Mar 1728 in Philadelphia Co, Pennsylvania.  
2 CONT *   3   ii. Wilhelm In Den Hoffen was born 1653 in Mettman, Rheinland, Preussen, Germany. He married Margret Loeckerss 8 Jul 1675 in Evangelical Church, Mettmann, Rheinland, Preussen, Germany 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm In Den Hoffen  b. 1653 is stated as a brother of Evert -  but also is  this same data base he  is given as the son of   Theis In Den Hoffen & Anna Gerdraut  as follows with other siblings of which Evert is not amongst as f
2 CONC ollows 
2 CONT
2 CONT  1. [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=declub&id=I3533 Theis In Den Hoffen]. He married Anna Gerdraut 1645.  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children of Theis In Den Hoffen and Anna Gerdraut are:
2 CONT   * 2   i. Petter In Den Hoffen was born 4 Mar 1662 in Mulheim, Germany. He married a b 28 Jul 1695 in Mulheim, Germany.  
2 CONT *   3   ii. Peter In Den Hoffen was born 1655 in Mettman, Rheinland, Preussen, Germany. He married Maria Paesthoff 25 Sep 1677 in Evangelical Church, Mettmann, Rheinland, Preussen, Germany. She was born 1655 in Mettman, Rheinland,
2 CONC  Preussen, Germany.  
2 CONT *   4   iii. [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=declub&id=I10514 Wilhelm In Den Hoffen ] was born 1653 in Mettman, Rheinland, Preussen, Germany. He married Margret Loeckerss 8 Jul 1675 in Evangelical Church
2 CONC , Mettmann, Rheinland, Preussen, Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT More Sources :
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/h/i/t/Robin-L-Hitzel/GENE22-0001.html  Descendants of Evert DeHaven ]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~danielcurtisphillips/p32.htm#i969 Phillips - Balkcom - Price - Wagner Family History]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=mckate&id=I1801 McCarter Family Tree ]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Evart InDenHoven, or Ten Heuven, arrived in America in 1684 and was part of William Penn's Dutch Quaker migration, mentioned several times in the book, "William Penn and the Dutch Quaker Migration to Pennsylvania," by William Hull
2 CONC  of Swarthmore College.
2 CONT
