2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham and propably Trijntje as well are buried at the Evansburg Mennonite Cemetery, in old Perkiomen Twp., Skippackville, Pa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham was the youngest of the three brothers who came from Krefeld on the ship [IT:Concord:IT] in 1683, as part of l3 Mennonite families who settled Germantown, Pa.  Their journey and impressions of the new land was described in
2 CONC  a letter by his brother Herman as follows, "The blessings of the Lord did attend us so that we had a wonderfully prosperous voyage....our number did not decrease ..but was increased by two - a son & a dau., the mothers were easy 
2 CONC in labor & soon well again.....The Indians show themselves very kind & friendly, we travel through the forest w/o the least fear of them.  Most of us have already our own habitations, and we have begun to spin flax." The brothers 
2 CONC bought 2000 acres, part around Germantown, and part along the Perkiomen.  They were weavers, and continued this industry in Germantown.  Abraham won a Governor's prize for the first and finest piece of linen woven in the state of 
2 CONC Pennsylvania. Religious convictions fluctuated between the Quaker factions and the Mennonites. While Abraham was a member of the German Quakers, he was one of the four signers of a significant anti-slavery petition, the first publ
2 CONC ic protest against slavery in North America submitted in 1688 to the mainstream English Quaker meetings.  It stated in part: "There is a saying that we shall do to all men like as we will be done ourselves; making no difference of
2 CONC  what generation, descent or colour they are." It went on to point out that most of them had come to America to escape oppression, so why then would they oppress those "who are of a black colour?"  The protest was considered and t
2 CONC abled as "so weighty that we think it not expedient to meddle with it here." Though suppressed, the idea did not die, and eventually Pennsylvania was the first state in the new nation to pass a law in 1780 to gradually abolish sla
2 CONC very within its borders, a century before the Civil War. Abraham eventually returned to the Mennonite faith.  Abraham was one of the original charter members of Germantown, one of the first committee-men, and a member of the Colon
2 CONC ial Assembly for several years, last in 1692.  He must have been a man of strong will (a trait that has not died out in his descendants), as he occasionally ran afoul of his neighbor's expectations, most notably in a matter of his
2 CONC  fences, deemed insufficient, a dispute over a fine assessed to him because his son Jacob "borrowed" a neighbor's horse without permission, and a debt for some purchased goods.  He may have been the inspiration for Whittier in his
2 CONC  poem "The Pennsylvania Pilgrim" which mentioned 'old Op den Graef'.  He and his wife sold their brick house and 828 acres in Germantown in 1704 and moved to the Perkiomen area, where he bought 1200 acres on the Perkiomen Creek, w
2 CONC here they lived out their lives.  Their children are Isaac, Jacob, [BO:Gertien, who married Richard ADDAMS:BO], Anneken, Margrit, and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham was the youngest of the three op den graeff brothers who came from krefeld, germany, on the ship concord. (See passenger list)
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham and his two brothers paid for 2000 acres of land in pennslyvnia, and purchased same from jacob telner (who had original land grants for the land from william penn, dated 3/9/1682. Eight hundred and twenty-eight acres of th
2 CONC is land was located in what is now germantown, pa., six miles from philadelphia. The eastern state penitentiry now stand on part of the original tract, at craterford, pa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham and his brothers transferred their weaving industry to germantown and were largely responsible for making it famous and it was claimedd that abraham was the most skilled of all germantown's "high german people and low dutc
2 CONC h" whose trade in weaving linnen cloth is much. Abraham won a governor's prize of 1500 lire for the first and finest piece of linen woven in the state of pennslyvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is much dispute concerning the relligious complexion of the original thirteen families from krefeld; mennonite or quaker? It is felt that they were memmonites. However, shortly after their settlement in america, abraham beca
2 CONC me a member of the quakers, also known as "the religious society of friends."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1688, abraham and his brother derick were two of the four persons who signed the "anti slavery" petition. This petition was known as the "germantown friends" protest against slavery, 1688, and was signed by derick up de graeff,
2 CONC  abraham up den graef and francis pastorius on 2/18/1688
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1689, abraham was one of the eleven men to whom william penn granted a charter for germantown. Therein, abraham is referred to as "abraham isaacs opte graef" and in the charter he was one of the six men appointed to serve as th
2 CONC e first "committee-men. He served as town "burgess" in 1692 and was a member of the colonial assembly in 1689, 1690 and 1692.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1689, abraham donated 50 acres of land to the "germantown friends" and thereon, they built, in 1693, their first meeting house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham fell from political favor in 1692, and his last years were marked by difficulties with the civil government. The fence-overseer condemned his fences in 1696, as being insufficient. In 1791, his son, jacob, "borrowed" a nei
2 CONC ghbor's horse (without permission) and was fined a half-crown while abraham was condenmed to pay the costs of the legal action involved. Abraham quarreled with the sheriff about these cost and two years later, did do "mightily abu
2 CONC se to the bailiff, in open court, and was fined two pounds and 10 shillings. In 1704, abraham was sued by a neighbor for money due on purchased goods and after a court action had the debt arbitrated. That same year, an old krefeld
2 CONC , germany neighbor and fellow pilgrim, veit or david scherkes, declared that "no honest man would be in the company of abraham." abraham sued him for slander, but lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham and his wife sold their brick house and 828 acres of land in germantown on about 5/16/1704m and moved to neighboring perkiomen area (now montgomery county) where he laid out the remaining 1200 acres of perkiomen creed. Abr
2 CONC aham died there.
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
