1 NOTE {geni:about_me} #[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Op_den_Graeff en.wikipedia.org - '''Op den Graeff'''...]  
2 CONT #[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_op_den_Graeff en.wikipedia.org - '''Abraham Op den Graeff''' - Isaac's father 1649-1731] 
2 CONT #[http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=REG&db=jwbittner&id=I2443 /wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com]  
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Updegraff (also spelled OpdenGraef) was born circa 1678 in probably Krefeld, Germany. He is not listed as a passenger on the ship CONCORD as he was under the age of 12 in 1683, and as such was counted only as half a "freight
2 CONC ." 
2 CONT
2 CONT After their arrival in Germantown, Isaac and his brother Jacob attended the Pastorius school. In 1731, Isaac inherited one-fourth of his father's 530 acres (132.5 acres) in what is now Montgomery County, Pennsylvania (Ref: Phila. 
2 CONC D.B. C, No. 2, Vol. 3, p. 95.) It would appear that this was about the time that Isaac was preparing to leave for Coventry Township in Chester County, Pennsylvania, as his name appears in the Coventry Township's tax list in 1732, 
2 CONC 1734, and 1735. His name is found spelled Uptigrave, Updegraft, and Updegraff on these tax lists. He was referred to by many as "Isaac of Coventry." (Ref: Name index to Chester County Tax Lists - 1699-1758 & 1775-1783, & Gilbert C
2 CONC ope's Collection).
2 CONT
2 CONT see Barbara Wentz' 2006 map for the land holding he received from his father in Providence township
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isaac was employed by the Potts family at their ironworks at Pine Forge and Colebrookdale. Isaac and his son, Jacob, crossed the Schuykill River to Chester County where Samuel Nutt was making iron at Coventry in partnership with 
2 CONC William Branson and Mordecai Lincoln, the great, great grandfather of President Abraham Lincoln. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the very earliest iron works in this country was the "Coventry Forge" established in 1716. This forge ran a sort of general store, selling and buying many things to and for families for many miles around. The furnace store 
2 CONC ledgers called the "Pott's Manuscripts" are in the possession of the Chester County Historical Society and at one time was in the possession of Governor Samuel W. Pennypacker (and Updegraff descendant), and historian and genealogi
2 CONC st of long ago. The ledgers are a very valuable source of information. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Updegraff had an account with the Potts from 1732 to 1745. Isaac died in August 1745. He married his wife Mary on possibly April 29, 1712.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Information published in 1988 indicates, Isaac's year of death, 1745, was on his gravestone in the Mennonite Cemetery in Skippackville. The stone had long since disappeared but letters from earlier genealogists had described
2 CONC  it in detail.
