1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Residence''': Fort Erie - 1785
2 CONT * '''Residence''': moved from Fort Erie to Charlotteville, settled on William's lot. - 1793
2 CONT ** Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-1/myheritage-family-trees?itemId=225707171-2-8315&action=showRecord MyHeritage Family Trees] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 19 2016, 
2 CONC 0:00:10 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=88443161
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Abraham SMITH   age: 72 
2 CONT     Born:       1737      in: 
2 CONT     Died: 1-Apr-1809      in: Charlotteville,Norfolk,ON,CAN               
2 CONT   Buried:                 in: BaptistCem,Vittoria,Norfolk,ON,CAN          
2 CONT      Ref:                     Occupation: wheelwright                     
2 CONT   Father:  
2 CONT   Mother: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham SMITH
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham SMITH was born in 1737 in Chester, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of the province of New York (Westchester County)
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH was a wheelwright by trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH married 1/w Abigail?
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH married 2/w Rachel (b. 1759/1760; Dutch) in 1770 in
2 CONT                   Westchester County, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH was enlisted in the third (North or Manor of
2 CONT                   Cortlandt) Regiment of the Westchester County Militia.
2 CONT                   [Ref. Vol. IV of Documents relating to the Colonial History of
2 CONT                   the State of New York]
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH was placed under arrest, because as a member of the
2 CONT                   local American militia, he had secretly helped Loyalists to reach
2 CONT                   Niagara.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   The first imprisonment -- after 1783 -- Abraham was confined for
2 CONT                   three months, part of which time was on board of a prison ship on
2 CONT                   the North River (the Hudson immediately above Manhattan).
2 CONT
2 CONT                   The second imprisonment -- July/Sept. 1786 -- Abraham was "taken
2 CONT                   up and confined in the American Army Provost guard (police force)
2 CONT                   for three weeks, thirteen days of which time your petitioner was
2 CONT                   loaded with heavy irons."
2 CONT
2 CONT                   1786--Abraham SMITH's property (1,113 acres and sawmill) near
2 CONT                   (about nine miles from) White Plains, Westchester County, New
2 CONT                   York was confiscated.
2 CONT                   [Ref. Extracts from Abraham SMITH's application for land grants
2 CONT                   in Canada; B.T. Holmes]
2 CONT
2 CONT                   1786 -- Abraham SMITH and his family were exiled from
2 CONT                   Revolutionary New York by the Provost Guard (separating him from
2 CONT                   his 1,113 acres and sawmill) who transported them and his
2 CONT                   portable property, which consisted of a big wagon, horses,
2 CONT                   valuables in packing cases and boxes and a rapidly dwindling
2 CONT                   supply of cash, across the Hudson River and deposited them (with
2 CONT                   advice of their arrival) on the shores of Revolutionary New
2 CONT                   Jersey. The place where they were landed was New Brunswick, New
2 CONT                   Jersey. They were promptly advised by Revolutionary New Jersey
2 CONT                   that their stay there would have to be very brief, or it would
2 CONT                   confiscate all their portable possessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham hid in a large freight box. When the family was accosted
2 CONT                   by the sergeant, they were intending to spend the next night in
2 CONT                   Somerville, New Jersey. Rachel pretended that Abraham had already
2 CONT                   gone on there. Abraham only spent one whole night and day in his
2 CONT                   box with his "provisions and a couple of jars of milk."
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham Smith arrived in Ontario,CAN in 1786 "by sworn statement."
2 CONT
2 CONT                   In the winter of 1786-1787 (with the consent of Revolutionary New
2 CONT                   Jersey) a large convoy (46 families) of United Empire Loyalist
2 CONT                   (UEL) refugees was being organized at Newton, Sussex County, New
2 CONT                   Jersey.  Almost all the members of this loyalist refugee convoy
2 CONT                   came from Sussex County, New Jersey, itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH and his family drove north in their wagon from
2 CONT                   Somerville, New Jersey to Newton, Sussex County, New Jersey and
2 CONT                   joined the convoy of Loyalist refugees, using their cash
2 CONT                   resources until Abraham had only seven shillings in his pocket.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   March 1787 the refugee convoy took off north for Niagara via
2 CONT                   Indian Trails through the wilderness --
2 CONT
2 CONT                   From Newton, Sussex County, New Jersey to the Finger Lake
2 CONT                   district of New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Then they turned west toward Niagara near present Syracuse, New
2 CONT                   York.  Here on May 15, 1787, young Abraham was born. [Ref.
2 CONT                   transcription of the gravestone]
2 CONT
2 CONT                   When the refugee column reached the Niagara River in the summer
2 CONT                   of 1787, about half the families were ferried across from Fort
2 CONT                   Niagara to Newark (Niagara on Lake Ontario, North or Lower end of
2 CONT                   the river) and half were ferried across from Buffalo, New York to
2 CONT                   Fort Erie (South or Upper end of the river).
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH family were ferried across the Niagara River about
2 CONT                   July 10, 1787 (May 15, 1787 plus eight weeks)(Owen, p. 43) from
2 CONT                   Buffalo, New York to Fort Erie where they pitched their tent in
2 CONT                   the wilderness.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH arrived in the province (Upper Canada) and brought
2 CONT                   with him a wife (Rachel) and nine children (five sons and four
2 CONT                   daughters) in the summer of 1787.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   1793 - The entire SMITH family joined William SMITH at Young's
2 CONT                   Creek Valley, in what was to become Charlotteville Township,
2 CONT                   Norfolk County, Ontario, Canada, then only an Indian village.
2 CONT                   The SMITH family was the first white family (pioneer settlers) in
2 CONT                   Norfolk County, Ontario, Canada.  At first the family lived as
2 CONT                   the Indians lived.  As soon as they were able, they built a
2 CONT                   substantial log house on the bank of Young's Creek.  Living in
2 CONT                   the wilderness was not easy. Land had to be cleared before seed
2 CONT                   could be sown and bear food. They had brought 2 cows with them,
2 CONT                   and the milk was precious to their livelihood. Game was
2 CONT                   plentiful, but guns and ammunition were very scarce.  Education
2 CONT                   was also scarce. At the time the pioneers needed education most,
2 CONT                   there were no schools to attend.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH built the first frame barn in the township.
2 CONT                   Some years later he constructed a horse-power mill for crushing
2 CONT                   grain.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   1797 - The Long Point Settlement school was the first school in
2 CONT                   Norfolk County.  William Pitt Gilbert came to the house of Mr.
2 CONT                   Abraham Smith, the first settler on the plains in the Township of
2 CONT                   Charlotteville, and solicited to be employed as a teacher.  He
2 CONT                   was then employed by Abraham Smith and eight other subscribers.
2 CONT                   Classes were opened in 1797 in a small outhouse belonging to
2 CONT                   Abraham Smith.  This was on Lot 15, Concession 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   The first students were Samuel Smith, William Smith, Jesse Smith,
2 CONT                   Rachel Smith, Abraham Smith, Solomon Smith, Hannah Smith, Ephraim
2 CONT                   Mitchell, Martin Mitchell, Francis L. Welch, Daniel Culver,
2 CONT                   Aquila M. Welch (others are listed).
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Shortly thereafter, the first schoolhouse was opened on Lot 15,
2 CONT                   Concession 4 on the south side of Young's Creek, Charlotteville.
2 CONT                   It was located by the side of the then new road which led from
2 CONT                   the place of Thomas Welch (at the forks of Young's Creek) to the
2 CONT                   mouth of said creek.
2 CONT                   [Ref. Bannister, J.A. "Early Educational History of Norfolk
2 CONT                   County." p. 52-53]
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH applied for land in 1797 in Charlotteville.
2 CONT
2 CONT                        (1)"...after being long imprisoned and all his property
2 CONT                   confiscated, came into this province with a wife and ten children
2 CONT                   in 1786 . . . your petitioner was long confined in the American
2 CONT                   War his estate in landed property to amount of 1113 acres was
2 CONT                   confiscated, and all his personal property taken from him, and
2 CONT                   when he came into this province in 1786 as above recited had only
2 CONT                   seven shillings left . . . 16 June 1795
2 CONT                        (2)"That your petitioner being a native of the province of
2 CONT                   New York in North America and having always been most strongly
2 CONT                   attached to the Crown and Government of Great Britain suffered
2 CONT                   much and lost by an act of confiscation on account of his loyalty
2 CONT                   a valuable landed property in the said Province of New York
2 CONT                   containing eleven hundred and thirteen acres with a sawmill and
2 CONT                   other valuable improvements thereon"
2 CONT                        "That your petitioner in the time of the late American
2 CONT                   Rebellion was taken up and confined in the American Provost
2 CONT                   Guard, for three weeks thirteen days of which time your
2 CONT                   petitioner was loaded with heavy irons and at another time though
2 CONT                   before the last mentioned, your petitioner was imprisoned for the
2 CONT                   space of three months part of which time your petitioner was
2 CONT                   confined on board of a prison ship in the North River."
2 CONT                        "That the charge exhibited against your petitioner and for
2 CONT                   which he suffered as aforesaid, was concealing and assisting
2 CONT                   Loyalists to proceed to Niagara."
2 CONT                        "That your petitioner arrived in this province in the year
2 CONT                   of our Lord one thousand seven hundred and eighty-six, and
2 CONT                   brought with him a wife and nine children viz. five sons and four
2 CONT                   daughters, and since the arrival of your petitioner in this
2 CONT                   province, it has pleased Divine Providence to favor him with one
2 CONT                   son and one daughter more . . . Feb. 6, 1797
2 CONT                        (3)"...Abraham Smith personally came and appeared before me
2 CONT                   Samuel Ryerse . . . and swore on the Holy Evangelist of Almighty
2 CONT                   God that he moved from the States to this Province in the year
2 CONT                   1786 and brought with him a wife, five sons and four daughters,
2 CONT                   names as follows: Isaac, Samuel, William, Jesse and Abraham-
2 CONT                   Abigail, Charity, Mary and Rachel." Feb. 7, 1797.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   [Ref. extracts from "Abraham Smith's" application for land grants
2 CONT                   in Canada; B.T. Holmes]
2 CONT
2 CONT                   17 Feb 1802 Abraham Smith petitioned for land in Norfolk County,
2 CONT                   Ontario, Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   17 May 1802 he was granted 400 acres, Lots 14 and 15 of
2 CONT                   Concession 5, Charlotteville township, Norfolk County, Ontario,
2 CONT                   Canada.
2 CONT                   [Ref. Land Patents as reported by a Mrs. Harold (Maxine) Davis
2 CONT                   letter, Belmont, Ontario 1985]
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH's will of Feb. 24, 1809 names his daughter, Hannah,
2 CONT                   and his wife, Rachel. Executors were: wife, Rachel, sons William
2 CONT                   and Abraham 2.
2 CONT
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH age 72 died April 11, 1809 in Charlotteville
2 CONT                   Township, Norfolk County, Ontario, Canada.
2 CONT                   Abraham SMITH was buried in the old Baptist Church Cemetery at
2 CONT                   Vittoria, Canada.
2 CONT                   Rachel SMITH lived on after his death with baby Abraham and
2 CONT                   visits from "Uncle Billy" and her other children for 22 years
2 CONT                   after his death.
2 CONT                   Rachel SMITH died in 1831 in Charlotteville Township, Norfolk
2 CONT                   County, Ontario, Canada.
2 CONT
