1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Peter DeHaven Jr was born in 1719. He owned a plantation close to Valley Forge, Pa. During the revolution, he furnished supplies to the Continental Army while they were camped at Valley Forge. He also operated thre
2 CONC e gun factories in and near Philadelphia. When it came to revolutionary armament in and about Philadelphia, Peter DeHaven was the key man. The Council of Safety of the Continental Congress ordered a powder mill be erected within 5
2 CONC 0 miles of Philadelphia for the purposes of providing needed gun powder and arms to the colonial troops. The site along French Creek was selected by Col. Biddle, Capt. Cowperthwaite and David Rittenhouse and purchased for $4,000 a
2 CONC nd construction of the Continental Powder Mill on French Creek began. Given the methods and tools of the time, the enormous size of the raceways and numerous buildings, Snyder’s Mill was up and running in an astonishing short peri
2 CONC od of time. By June of that year the powder mill was operational with a well known gunsmith Peter DeHaven serving as manager. The mill produced about 2 tons of gun powder per 1 week. By January 3, 1777 Peter DeHaven reported to th
2 CONC e Council that he had nineteen men at guns at French Creek  Saturday, February 3, 1777 was a busy year for Dehaven and his assembly of gunsmiths and powder makers. In spite of impediments, production appeared to continue but the s
2 CONC tuff of war was always in short supply for the militias and the Continental Army. On September 7, Dehaven wrote an urgent letter to Council: We have got some information that there is a part of Mr. Howe's army within four miles of
2 CONC  Downing's Town and I believe they intend for our magazine and we are in a very poor situation for defending it. I suggest moving the powder by wagon toward Reading, but to what place I am a stranger. This letter found the Council
2 CONC  of Safety in its own whirlwind, as they were hastily gathering their own papers in preparation to flee the city of Philadelphia themselves and move inland. One factory was captured by the British and another was moved to avoid ca
2 CONC pture. Peter was also a member of the Philadelphia Committee of Safety. He owned tanneries which provided shoes for the Army.  He died on 12 Nov 1815 in Philadelphia, Montgomery Co, Pa. and is buried in the Christ Church Burial Gr
2 CONC ound.  Below is a marker in front of his tombstone along with his wife Sarah.
2 CONT
