2 NOTE USGS map is on page 26 of 62 at https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/83ea138d-241e-4dba-b4dc-f8075ff591fa
2 NOTE Due to the method in which Pikeland Township was created, it is generally difficult to trace land ownership to an individual
3 CONT owner prior to circa 1789 (Cremers 1989:25-39). The HRSF suggests that an early owner or occupier of the property may
3 CONT have been a member of the Lightfoot family, an early settler and prominent landholder in the township, but this is not
3 CONT confirmed. Later owners of the tract apparently include Casper Snyder and Michael Hartman and in the nineteenth
3 CONT century the Evans family. Currently the house is owned by the Cava family.
2 NOTE The site of the Continental Powder Works at French Creek (hereafter, Powder Works, or powder works complex) consists
3 CONT of land generally extending from South Rapps Dam Road, near the Rapps Dam Covered Bridge crossing the creek, in
3 CONT East Pikeland Township, Chester County, Pennsylvania (Figure 1), north of SR113/Kimberton Road. The Continental
3 CONT Powder Works was an industrial complex producing gun powder and armaments for the American Revolution during 1776
3 CONT and 1777. The historic “Powder Mill Tract” consisted of 119+ acres.
3 CONT 1 (See Figure 7) The land slopes downward from the
3 CONT southern and western corners of tract to the floodplain of French Creek. The Powder Works’ identified resources are
3 CONT adjacent to the raceway that follows the base of the slope within the tract. The Powder Works site contains six identified
3 CONT contributing features and is believed to contain additional as-yet unidentified features. The identified features include an
3 CONT extant building (Cava House), the water power system structure and the associated French Creek (an uncounted
3 CONT landscape feature), a historic road bed structure, and stamping mill, graining mill, and drying house sites. Together, these
3 CONT inter-related features form one overall site known as the Powder Works site. The majority of the floodplain along the
3 CONT Creek is now heavily wooded. The central and southern uphill portions of the tract contain 16 non-contributing
3 CONT buildings/building complexes (National Guard Armory complex, East Pikeland Township complex, and nineteenth- and
3 CONT twentieth-century residences), a non-contributing railroad bed structure, and four non-contributing sites that post-date and
3 CONT are not related to the Powder Works. At the eastern edge of the site is the individually-listed Rapp’s Covered Bridge,
3 CONT which post-dates the period of the Powder Works. As it is individually listed, it is not included in the Resource Count. Not
3 CONT all of the land historically included within the Powder Works property is included with this nomination. Acreage that has
3 CONT been compromised by modern residential development (see Figure 5, cf. Figure 2) has been excluded from the boundary.
3 CONT If in the future associated features are identified and retain integrity, the nomination may be amended, if appropriate, to
3 CONT include those features. It is anticipated that most unidentified features will be found within the proposed current boundary.
3 CONT The property currently nominated includes approximately 98 acres.
3 CONT It is not known how much of the land purchased by the Committee of Safety in 1776 under authority of the Continental
3 CONT Congress was actually used for the industrial operations needed to produce guns or powder. Over the course of
3 CONT approximately a year-and a-half, from April 1776 until September 1777, the site consisted of (at least) a dam on French
3 CONT Creek, a hand-dug mill race, a stamping mill, a graining mill, a saltpeter house, four drying houses, a powder magazine,
3 CONT and at least one residence for the manager. A stamping house is the building in which the ingredients of gunpowder
3 CONT (including charcoal, sulfur, and potash) were mixed. The gunpowder was polished and sorted by grain size in the graining
3 CONT mill. Saltpeter (potassium nitrate, a major component of gunpowder) was manufactured in the saltpeter house. Finished
3 CONT gunpowder would be dried in the drying houses. After drying, the finished powder was stored in the magazine or
3 CONT storehouse building prior to shipping. Additionally, the Powder Works site is believed to have housed a gun factory,
3 CONT which included a boring/grinding mill to finish gun barrels, various “smith” shops and support buildings, and a barracks to
3 CONT house local militia. Those features have not yet been identified.
