2 CONT Samuel Dehaven  who departed this life | February 19, 1815 in the 91st Year of his Age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War Soldier. He is variously identified as a weaver, a farmer, and a yeoman. He owned a farm in Whitepain Township. He loaned $17,500 to Washington for relief of the Revolutionary Soldiers at Valley Forge. He was a p
2 CONC rivate, 1st Class in Captain Abraham Wentz's 2nd Company, 6th Battalion. He is also identified as a member of the Philadelphia County Militia, 1st Battalion, 5th Company. He was the husband of Susanna Yocum Spaulding.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==WIKITREE==
2 CONT Samuel Dehaven (1724 - 1815)
2 CONT Born 8 Mar 1724 in Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 
2 CONT Died 19 Feb 1815 at age 90 in Upper Merion, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA  
2 CONT Samuel Dehaven 
2 CONT Son of Peter De Haven and Sidonia (Levering) de Haven 
2 CONT Brother of Edward Dehaven, William Dehaven, Magdalena Deborah (Dehaven) Supplee, John B. De Haven, Peter DeHaven, Elizabeth (Dehaven) Yocum, Gerhard Dehaven, Mary De Haven, Jacob De Haven, John DeHaven and Abraham Dehaven  
2 CONT Husband of Susanna (Yocum) Dehaven — married [date unknown] [location unknown]
2 CONT Father of Isaac DeHaven
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Samuel was born on 8 March 1724 in Philadelphia, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA. He was the son of Peter De Haven and Sidonia (Levering) de Haven.[1] 
2 CONT Samuel died at the age of 90 on 19 February 1815 in Lower Marion, Pennsylvania.[1] He was buried in Christ Church Upper Merion Espiscopal Cemetery (Old Swedes' Church), Bridgeport, Montgomery, Pennsylvania.[2] 
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT Lower Marion, Whitpain, Old Gulph Mills, Pa.
2 CONT Sources
