1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Grietjen Pieters Dohrs=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth:	circa 1620 in Lower Rhineland, Heiliges Römisches Reich Deutscher Nation  Citation_note
2 CONT * Death:	November 19, 1683 in Philadelphia, Philadelphia County, Province of Pennsylvania, Colonial America 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Pieters-764
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Margaret Greitjen Pieters''', daughter of Peter Cuypers Doors and Lysgen Grietes, has roots in the region now known as Nordrhein-Westfalen, Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greitjen was born around 1620 to a Mennonite family in the Lower Rhine area of the Holy Roman Empire. The Low German her community spoke had as much in common with Dutch as High German. She married Isaak Herman Op den Graeff and t
2 CONC hey had three sons and two daughters. Their sons were all weavers.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the late 1670s, the Op den Graeffs were part of a thriving Mennonite community in Krefeld, not far from the Dutch border. Like many others in the community, they were drawn to the ideas promoted by missionaries for the Society 
2 CONC of Friends and converted to Quakerism. They found themselves no longer as happily tolerated in Krefeld as they had been previously.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greitjen was widowed in 1679 and her sons and various other families in the community were drawn towards the opportunity of a new life in a new province with complete religious freedom. This was being offered by another Quaker, Wi
2 CONC lliam Penn. The father-in-law of one of her sons purchased land directly from Penn, and the three Op den Graeff brothers all agreed to invest and emigrate as settlers of the new land.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1683, Greitjen joined her daughter, her three sons and their wives, and ten other Mennonite/Quaker families from Krefeld on a journey initially to England. There they had arranged (through their contact Franz Past
2 CONC orius) passage on a ship contracted by James Claypoole, another Quaker who had purchased land from Penn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite arriving a little later than planned, they were able to embark the schooner "Concord" at Gravesend. [1][2][3][4] After a 75-day journey, the ship arrived in Philadelphia in October, 1683.
2 CONT
2 CONT The op den Graeff family was one of the original 13 families which settled Germantown, Pennsylvania on the land purchased from Penn, just to the north of Philadelphia, and played an important role in the settlement's management an
2 CONC d governance.[5], [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sadly, Greitjen passed away in November 1683 barely a month after arriving. Three years later, with the settlement thriving and growing, her children were joined by their sister Maria and her family in Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Adolphus /Op den Graeff/''' b. aft. 1636, d. bef. 1654
2 CONT # '''Herman Isaacs /Op den Graeff/''' b. 1642, married 1st, Liesbet Isacks van Bebber and 2nd, Debora van Bebber, d. 1701
2 CONT # '''Dirck Isaacks /Op de Graeff/''' b. bef. 1642, married Noleken Vijten on Mar 20, 1681, d. before May 24, 1697. No children
2 CONT # '''Maria op den Graeff''' - b. ca. 1643, married Johannes Yelles Peter Cassel, III, d. November 08, 1728
2 CONT # '''Abraham Isaacs /Op den Graeff/'''+ b. ca. 1646, married Catharine (Trijnte) Jansen on Jul 23, 1679, d. Mar 27, 1731
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter also seen, Margaret (Op den Graeff) Schumacher (abt. 1675 - 1748), seems to better fit the next generation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: This wiki profile had the date of birth listed as 28 Feb 1616 and the date of death listed as 17 Jan 1679 when it was adopted by [[Friebel-9|Donna Storz]]. There were no sources for the exact dates provided.
2 CONT
