2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes for Herman Isaacs Op den GRAEFF:=
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman Isaacs op den Graeff, descendant of an old Mennonite family, left the Mennonite fold in 1679 to become a Quaker. The next year he co-authored a pamphlet about religious persecution which was published in Amsterdam and Rotte
2 CONC rdam. In Maintaining the Right Fellowship, Mennonite historian John L. Ruth tells the story of Herman's own persecution: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ‘’The [Krefeld] Reformed Synod received complaints that these former Mennonites no longer practiced baptism or the Lord's summer (claiming these were entirely spiritual matters), held separate meetings, and for some irritating rea
2 CONC son would not doff their hats even when greeted in the street. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Just how angry this made some Krefelders was evidenced by the expulsion of the new Quaker, weaver Herman op den Graeff, and five other persons by armed men who led them out of the city. After the Quakers had cautiously returned,t
2 CONC hey were re-expelled, with a local deputy swearing by his soul's salvation that if they came back again he would personally see them whipped and branded. After they had nevertheless slipped unobtrusively back into their homes, sti
2 CONC ll another Krefeld Quaker, Johannes Bleijckers, who failed to tip his hat to two of his neighbors, was thrown down, kicked and dragged by his hair until he was too seriously injured to walk. So we will not be surprised to find bot
2 CONC h Herman op den Graeff and Johannes Bleijckers, three years later, sailing toward America in search of another society." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman was one of the original 13 heads of household who settled Germantown. The small group of German Quakers arrived in Philadelphia 6 Oct 1683 on the Concord after a ?? month voyage from Gravesend. Herman came with his wife Lis
2 CONC bet van Bebber, his mother, his two brothers, Abraham and Dirck, and his sister Margaret. The op den Graeffs were evidently a family of means, since they were able to buy 2000 acres in Pennsylvania before sailing. Herman acted as 
2 CONC Pennsylvania land agent for Jacob Telner and Dirck Sipman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman Isaacs op den Graeff, descendant of an old Mennonite family, left the Mennonite fold in 1679 to become a Quaker. The next year he co-authored a pamphlet about religious persecution which was published in Amsterdam and Rotte
2 CONC rdam. In MAINTAINING THE RIGHT FELLOWSHIP, Mennonite historian John L. Ruth tells the story of Herman's own persecution: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "The [Krefeld] Reformed Synod received complains that these former Mennonites no longer practiced baptism or the Lord's supper (claiming these were entirely spiritual matters), held separate meetings, and for some irritating reaso
2 CONC n would not doff their hats even when greeted in the street. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Just how angry this made some Krefelders was evidenced by the expulsion of the new Quaker, weaver Herman Op den Graeff, and five other persons by armed men who led them out of the city. After the Quakers had cautiously returned, 
2 CONC they were re-expelled, with a local deputy swearing by his soul's salvation that if they came back again he would personally see them whipped and branded. After they had nevertheless slipped unobtrusively back into their homes, st
2 CONC ill another Krefeld Quaker, Johannes Bleijckers, who failed to tip his hat to two of his neighbors, was thrown down, kicked and dragged by his hair until he was too seriously injured to walk. So we will not be surprised to find bo
2 CONC th Herman op den Graeff and Johannes Bleijckers, three years later, sailing toward America in search of another society." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman was one of the original 13 heads of household who settled in Germantown. The small group of German Quakers arrived in Philadelphia 6 Oct. 1683 on the Concord after a ?? month voyage from Gravesend. Herman came with his wife
2 CONC  Lisbet van Bebber, his mother, his two brothers, Abraham and Dirck, and possibly his sister Margaret. (Some researchers say yes, some say no on Margaret.) The Op den Graeffs were evidently a family of means since they were able t
2 CONC o buy 2000 acres in Pennsylvania before sailing. Herman acted as Pennsylvania agent for Jacob Telner and Dirck Sipman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman had the third lot on the west side of the Germantown main street, between his two brothers, who were also weavers. When William Penn granted a charter to Germantown in 1689, Herman was one of the 11 incorporators. He was a 
2 CONC burgess and the first "towne president". He signed a petition against a tax bill in 1692; the next year he signed the petition supporting George Keith in the controversy which split the Pennsylvania Quakers. His property was asses
2 CONC sed for #80.1.5. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1684 Herman wrote a letter to Europe describing the trip over as "a wonderfully prosperous voyage". As he wrote how cattle eat in the woods in spite of the cold, Indians entered his makeshift house. According to Ruth, he "took 
2 CONC the hand of one, closed it around the pen, and went on with his letter for a line or two: '...in the town have one lot seven rods wide, three acres in size, also forty acres....'" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman's wife Lisbet died in 1691. Some writers (William Hull, William Penn and the Dutch Quaker Migration, and Samuel Pennypacker, Settlement of Germantown) say Herman died childless in 1701, leaving his estate to his brother Abr
2 CONC aham, but the consensus is that he he married Lisbet's sister Deborah and moved to Kent Co, DE on 29 Sep 1701. A "Herman Updegrove" died there in 1708, leaving his estate to wife Deborah and mentioning, though not naming, minor ch
2 CONC ildren. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT correspondence w/Andree Hoeman, Columbus, NE 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hull, WILLIAM PENN & THE DUTCH QUAKER MIGRATION TO PENNSYLVANIA (Swarthmore College 1935), pp 196-97, 208-09, 216-17, 403, 416-17 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Pennypacker, SETTLEMENT OF GERMANTOWN Benjamin Bloom NY 1970 (1899) 
2 CONT
2 CONT John L. Ruth, MAINTAINING THE RIGHT FELLOWSHIP (Herald Press: Scottsdale, PA 1984), p 48 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clyde Updegraff Shank, Manuscripts, PA State Library, PA Genealogical Society 
2 CONT
2 CONT CALENDAR OF KENT CO DE PROBATE RECORDS 1680-1800 (DE Archive Commission 1944), p 30: ARdch Vol A51, p 198; Reg of Wills, Liber B, folio 67 
2 CONT
2 CONT "We whose names are to these presents subscribed, do hereby certify unto all whom it 
2 CONT
2 CONT may concern, that soon after our arrival in this province of Pennsylvania, in October, 1683, 
2 CONT
2 CONT to our certain knowledge, Herman op den Graff, Dirk op den Graff, and Abraham op den 
2 CONT
2 CONT Graff, as well as we ourselves, in the cave of Francis Daniel Pastorius, at Philadelphia, did 
2 CONT
2 CONT cast lots for the respective lots which they and we then began to settle in Germantown; and 
2 CONT
2 CONT the said Graffs (three brothers) have sold their several lots, each by himself, this 29 November, 
2 CONT
2 CONT A. D. 1709. Lenart Arets, Jan Lenson, Thomas Hundus, William Streygert, 
2 CONT
