2 CONT Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania, Will Book H. Page 528:  Will of Richard Adams, written Feb. 1, 1747, probated March 24, 1747 In the name of God, Amen:  I, RICHARD ADAMS, of Providence in the County of Philadelphia, yeoman, bein
2 CONC g very sick and weak in the Body, but of a Sound Disposing mind and memory, Considering the uncertainty of this Transitory Life and the Certainty of Death, Do make and Ordain this my Last Will and Testament in manner and form foll
2 CONC owing (viz)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  I give and Bequeath to my Eldest Daughters children,  SUSANNAH Kistard, late wife of Conrad Kistard, DECEASED the sum of Ten Pounds Lawful Money of Pennsylvania, to be Equally Divided between them, Share and Share alike, to be
2 CONC  paid within 12 months after my decease.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  I give and Bequeath unto my daughter Catherine, Widow of John Morris, the sum of Ten Pounds of like lawful money to be paid by my Executors within Twelve months after my decease.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  I give and Bequeath unto my Daughter Mary, Widow of Israel Morris, the sum of Ten Pounds of Like Money aforesaid to be paid within Twelve months after my decease.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  I give and Bequeath unto my daughter Margaret, now Wife of Paul Casselberry, the Sum of Ten Pounds Lawful Money aforesaid to be paid within Twelve Months by my Executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  I give and Bequeath unto my daughter Elizabeth, now Wife of Thos. Bull, the sum of Ten Pounds Lawful Money aforesaid to be paid within Twelve Months after my decease by my Executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  I give and Bequeath unto my daughter Ann, the sum of Ten Pounds Lawful Money aforesaid to be paid to her, or her husband Jacob Umstatt by my Executors within Twelve Months after my decease.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  I give and Bequeath unto my Son Abrams Children the sum of Ten Pounds Lawful Money aforesaid to be paid them or the survivor of them, Equally Share and Share alike (viz) Ann and Abigail, to be paid within Twelve Months after m
2 CONC y decease by my Executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.  Imprimise:  I give and Bequeath and Devise unto my two sons, viz., William and Isaac, the moiety, or half-part, of all the Remainder of my estate after the above Legacies, Debts and funeral Charges is paid, to be equally Divid
2 CONC ed between them Share and Share alike.  That is to say, each one is to have a quarter part to hold to them, their Heirs and Assigns for ever.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.  Imprimise:  I give, Bequeath and Devise unto my well beloved wife Alse and youngest Daughter Hannah the other Moiety, or half-part, of my estate after the aforesaid Debts and Legacies is paid and Discharged as aforesaid to be 
2 CONC equally Divided between my said Wife and Daughter Hannah Husband Owen Evans to hold them their Heirs and Assigns for ever, each one quarter part.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.  And Further:  It is my will that if Either of my aforesaid two sons William and Isaac shall be able and have a mind to keep my Plantation and Lands situated in the Township aforesaid, containing one hundred and fifty acres, I
2 CONC  give, Devise and Bequeath the same to one of them to hold to them, their Heirs and Assigns for Ever. Provided they pay the three parts in four of the value thereof to my other Son that shall not Chuse to keep the said Lands and t
2 CONC o my wife and Daughter Hannah or her aforesaid Husband Owen Evans, to each of them a quarter part as aforesaid within two years after my decease, otherwise I order and Impower my Executors, or the survivor of them, to Sell and Con
2 CONC vey my Plantation and Lands to the Highest Bidder or for the Best price that can be got for the same, and the Money ariseing therefrom to be Divided in four Equal parts after my debts and seven first Legacies are paid and discharg
2 CONC ed aforesaid (viz) one Quarter part to my said Wife, and one to my Daughter Hannah or her Husband Owen Evans, and one to my son William and the other Quarter part to my son Isaac.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11.  Lastly, I do make and ordain my aforesaid two sons and my said son-in-law Owen Evans to be Executors of this my Last Will and Testament, Disallowing, Revoking and Disannulling all other former Wills, Legacies and Executors by
2 CONC  me in any wise before this time named or bequeathed, ratifying and confirming this and no other to be my Last Will and Testament.  In Witness whereof I have hereunto set my Hand and Seal this First Day of February Anno Domini one
2 CONC  Thousand seven hundred and forty seven (eight). Richard  (X)  Adams  his mark Witnesses: Richard Bull James Betsen Peter Tyson Salford, March 24, 1747, James Betsen and Peter Tyson declared they saw and heard Richard Adams declar
2 CONC e the same to be his will.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Adams family were Anglicans, and early members of St. James Church, Perkiomen.   Richard Adams had inherited from his Uncle Nicholas in 1725 the property his grandfather Richard Adams I bought from William Penn in 1681. It is 
2 CONC not clear when Richard  came to America, or if his uncle Nicholas was here or in England when he died. Richard was certainly here in 1698 and married to Gertien Op den Graef, as her father Abraham deeded him as his son-in-law 91 a
2 CONC cres of land that year.  Richard's signature appears on the Petition of the Inhabitants of Colebrookdale of Berks Co. in 1728 for 'relief of suffering at the hands of the Ingians'.  The area they lived in was called Falkner's Swam
2 CONC p, near Goschenhoppen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard changed the spelling of his last name to Addams to keep his line distinct.  Richard and Gertien had nine children, Susanna, Catherine, Mary, [BO:Margaret, who married Paul CASTLEBERRY:BO], Ann, Elizabeth, William, Abraham,
2 CONC  and Isaac.  By his second wife, Alitia (Alse) Withers, he had a daughter Hannah.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT NAME-variations: ADDAMS Richard changed the spelling of his last name to Addams to keep his line distinct from the other Adams families.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Adams family were Anglicans, and early members of St. James Church, Perkiomen. Richard Adams inherited the property his grandfather Richard Adams I bought from William Penn in 1681. Richard inherited it from his Uncle Nicholas
2 CONC  in 1725. It is not clear when Richard came to America, or if his uncle Nicholas was here or in England when he died. Richard was certainly here in 1698 and married to Gertien Op den Graef, as her father Abraham deeded him, as his
2 CONC  son-in-law, 91 acres of land that year. Richard's signature appears on the Petition of the Inhabitants of Colebrookdale of Berks Co. in 1728 for 'relief of suffering at the hands of the Ingians'. The area they lived in was called
2 CONC  Falkner's Swamp, near Goschenhoppen."
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
