1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Information from Lloyd D. Ellis:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=dubart&id=I441
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: I441 
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Robert ELLIS Jr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT RFN: 441 
2 CONT
2 CONT Change Date: 27 JUN 2006 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sex: M 1 2 Quality: 0 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 3 4 Quality: 0 1758 in Caernarvon twp 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the Ellis Bible shows place of birth as Franklin County, VA, he most likely was born in Pennsylvania prior to the family moving to Loudoun Co. VA in the 1760`s. He moved to Franklin County in the 1780`s. Franklin did not 
2 CONC become a County until 1786. This was Lunenburg Co. and was sub - divided in 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lunenburg ( 1746 ) encompassed this county as well as others. This was all part of the " Southern expansion" designated the Lunenbury Count.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechlenburg - 1765
2 CONT
2 CONT Halifax - 1752
2 CONT
2 CONT Pittsylvania - 1767
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Patrick, Franklin, most of Bedford and a part of Appomattox
2 CONT
2 CONT Counties formed in 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref. Hand book of genealogists 7th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Change Date: 11 AUG 1996 
2 CONT
2 CONT Event: war 1776 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Ellis Cavalry in Rev. War. nbll (War Dept.) Name appears on Army register but had not received bounty land. It is believed that Robert served (along with Isaac Dehaven*) in the 1st Regiment of Light Dragoons formed in 1778 
2 CONC and captained by Maj. Henry Lee, better known as "Light Horse Harry Lee" ** (the father of Robert E. Lee, commanding General of the CSA). The regiment was also called "Lee`s Legion" and "Lee`s Partisan Corp". No record of Robert E
2 CONC llis in this Regt.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Wife was Ann (Nancy) Jones. [Wm. & Mary Jones show no dau. Nancy. She is believed to have been Elisabeth Ann (Nancy) Ellis, Roberts sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT ** Light Dragoons & Partisan Corps
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.army.mil/cmh-pg/lineage/mounted.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 2d PARTISAN CORPS (Lee's Legion)
2 CONT
2 CONT LINEAGE
2 CONT
2 CONT [Wright, Continental Army, pp. 348-349]
2 CONT
2 CONT Authorized 8 June 1776 in the Virginia State Troops as the 5th Troop of Light Horse Redesignated 25 June 1776 as the 5th Troop, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Light Horse Organized in summer 1776 at Williamsburg with recruits from northern Virginia (Virginia Light Horse adopted 25 November 1776 into the Continental Army, assigned to the Main Army, and redesignated as the 1st Continental
2 CONC  Light Dragoon Regiment)
2 CONT
2 CONT Withdrawn 7 April 1778 from the 1st Continental Light Dragoon Regiment and expanded to form Lee's Corps of Partisan Light Dragoons, an element of the Main Army, to consist of two troops recruited at large
2 CONT
2 CONT Expanded 28 May 1778 to consist of three troops
2 CONT
2 CONT (Captain Allen McLane's Company [see Annex] assigned 13 July 1779 as the 4th Troop)
2 CONT
2 CONT Expanded and redesignated 14 February 1780 as Lee's Legionary Corps, to consist of three mounted and three dismounted troops 
2 CONT
2 CONT Relieved 31 October 1780 from the Main Army and assigned to the Southern Department.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reorganized and redesignated 1 January 1781 as the 2d Partisan Corps, to consist of three mounted and three dismounted troops
2 CONT
2 CONT Disbanded 15 November 1783 at Winchester, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT ANNEX
2 CONT
2 CONT Authorized 13 January 1777 as Captain Allen McLane's Company, Patton's Additional Continental Regiment, an element of the Main Army 
2 CONT
2 CONT Organized 14 February-23 April 1777 at Dover, Delaware, with recruits from Delaware (Patton's Additional Continental Regiment assigned 22 May 1777 to the 4th Virginia Brigade, an element of the Main Army; relieved 22 July 1778 fro
2 CONC m the 4th Virginia Brigade and assigned to the Highlands Department)
2 CONT
2 CONT Withdrawn 16 December 1778 from Patton's Additional Continental Regiment and aligned to the Delaware Regiment, an element of the 3d Virginia Brigade of the Main Army
2 CONT
2 CONT Withdrawn 13 July 1779 from the Delaware Regiment and assigned to Lee's Corps of Partisan Light Dragoons as the 4th (Dismounted) Troop
2 CONT
2 CONT ENGAGEMENTS
2 CONT
2 CONT [Wright, Continental Army, p. 349]
2 CONT
2 CONT Northern New Jersey
2 CONT
2 CONT Defense of Philadelphia
2 CONT
2 CONT Philadelphia-Monmouth
2 CONT
2 CONT New York 1779
2 CONT
2 CONT New Jersey 1779
2 CONT
2 CONT New Jersey 1780
2 CONT
2 CONT Defense of the Carolinas
2 CONT
2 CONT Greene's Campaign
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain McLane's Company additionally served in: Iroquois 1779
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 19 MAY 1820 in Sumner Co.,, TN 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: 1820 Sumner Co.,, TN 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT About 7 miles N.N.E. of Gallatin in dist. 10 towards Union Hill, on Hwy 31 E; then 7/10 mi. northwest on Reese Rd. Cemetery is approx. 350 yds. northeast of the road on Reese property. Others of Ellis and Gwin family may be buried
2 CONC  there.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ben notes: Former US Highway 31 E is the old Gallatin Pike, presently designated Highway 6. I haven't been able to locate Reese Road - believe its name has changed. Likely suspects are Rock Bridge Road, Dry Fork Creek Road, and J
2 CONC ames Penn Road, all around 7 miles on the Gallatin Pike from city center, and all branching northwest of the Pike. Dry Fork Creek has a possible site at 0.7 mile, 350 yards northeast from the road, but it's not conclusive from Goo
2 CONC gle Earth inspection. There was a Dr. Reese who died in 1939 on Dry Fork Creek; his burial was in the Gallatin cemetery and not in a private cemetery. But that too does not exclude the existence of an earlier burial site on his pr
2 CONC operty. Suspected site at:  36°27'33.31"N, 86°21'15.91"W.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was named in order as the third son. Loudoun County Deed book pp. 260-264 Indenture dated April 12, 1785 James Jones (joiner) sells about 170 acres to Robert Ellis (farmer) for £ 100 money of Virginia. Original property of 
2 CONC Wm. Jones deceased, willed to wife Mary Jones (Roberts Mother ?) Robert sold this land to George Emrey for £ 175. Deed dated May 11, 1788.
2 CONT
2 CONT N.B. The Ellis family were in Va. by 1760`s. Loudoun County, VA Personal Property Tax Roll for 1787, List A, p. 26, below Abraham and Isaac Dehaven, Robert Ellis is listed as self employed with 4 horses and 6 head of cattle. The 1
2 CONC 758-1786 Index to the tithetables for Loudoun Co., VA, p. 42 show a Mary Jones (Roberts Mother ?) in Shelburne Parish 1771-72, 75 and 1782. The Tavenner Records show "Robert 1775 tithe Shelburne with Mrs. Mary Jones." She was the 
2 CONC wife of Wm. Jones deceased in 1772. On same page "Robert E (.....) son to 19: 1785-88 had 170 a Lost Cos: m Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Franklin County Deeds:
2 CONT
2 CONT Deed book 2, p. 240 May 25, 1791 Robert Ellis buys 230 acres from Thomas Parker & John Davies for £ 60. Land on the south branch of Gills Creek. Neighbors Talbot and Picklesheimer. Per Franklin County Will Book 1, p. 228 Robert El
2 CONC lis is mentioned as one of the Executors of a will signed July 1, 1803. Note, Samuel Duvall also mentioned as well as a John Coleman. Earliest proven date for Robert & Sarah in Sumner County, TN:
2 CONT
2 CONT Deed book 5, 572-573 September 17, 1807 Robert Ellis & wife Sarah of Sumner Co., TN sell the above 230 acres in Franklin County, VA to Jesse Dillon for £ 110.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deed book 3 pp. 97-99 February 1, 1794 Robert Ellis buys 304 acres from Phelemon Sutherland for £ 35. Land on the grassy fork of Bull Run. See Franklin County Historical Society map of early settlers, showing property of Robert El
2 CONC lis and neighbor Isaac Dehaven. Also nearby the Ryherd`s land. This property is in Union Hall (Community) some 15 miles from Rocky Mount, east on hwy 40 which cuts across 2 branches of Bull Run. The property may have lain on both 
2 CONC sides of Hwy 40. Take Kay Fork Rd. to circle Bull Run.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deed book 3, pp 249-250 April 6, 1796 Robert Ellis "sells" the 304 acres to (his Mother ?) Mary Jones for £ 35. Sumner Co., TN tax rolls show a Robert Ellis with 640 acres on Aug. 25, 1789.
2 CONT
2 CONT Subj: Re: [ELLIS-L] Re: William N. Ellis
2 CONT
2 CONT Date: 8/21/01 10:18:08 PM Central Daylight Time
2 CONT
