1 NAME Peter /Supplee/
2 GIVN Peter
2 SURN Supplee
2 _MARNM Supplee
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Bethel Hill Methodist Episcopal Church Cemetery (Plot Row 6  Grave 13)
2 ADDR
3 CITY Lansdale
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 SEP 1745
2 PLAC now Montgomery County
2 ADDR
3 CITY Whitpain Township
3 STAE PA
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 JAN 1778
2 ADDR
3 CITY Valley Forge
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Peter and his wife were living upon this homestead property when the Revolutinary war broke out.  September 12, 1777, Peter volunteered as a soldier in the Revolutionary army, joining Captain Charles Wilson Peale's
2 CONC  company of Philadelphia Militia. Captain Peale's company belonged to the Fourth Battalion of the Philadelphia Regiment of Foot.  Captain Peale was friends with General George Washington, and painted a portrait of him at Valley Fo
2 CONC rge which is now the property of the Pennsylvania States Normal School as West Chester, PA.
2 CONT
2 CONT The old Supplee homestead property is historic, ground, a Revolutionary landmark. General Washington's army was twice encamped upon this property in the month of October, 1777, when that distinguished soldier led his troops from P
2 CONC ennypacker's Mills, at Perkiomen, about October 1, 1777, to give battle to the British army at Germantown. He came with the army to this place, and was encamped there on the second and third days of October. Whilst the army was en
2 CONC camped upon the farm, General Washington and General Greene had their headquarters in the Supplee dwelling house and October 3rd General Washington and his officers held a council f war on the lawn in front of the house. That afte
2 CONC rnoon the army broke camp and marched to Germantown. The battle of Germantown took place the next day, October 4, 1777. Owing to a dense fog existing at that time, that battle proved disastrous to the American Army. It was defeate
2 CONC d, and on October 5 retreated to Pennypacker's Mills, on the same road by which it had advanced to Germantown, two days before. After resting at Pennypacker's Mills until October 8, Washington advanced his army to Towamencin towns
2 CONC hip, remaining there until October 15. The army again came to the Supplee homestead, and was encamped there from October 15 to October 21. The army then advanced to Whitpain township, remaining there until November 1. On November 
2 CONC 2, Washington advanced his army to Whitemarsh, and took a position there, intending to give battle to the enemy. On December 3 General Howe, with the British army, marched out from Philadelphia and took a position at Chestnut Hill
2 CONC , some three miles distant from Washington's army. After the armies had confronted each other in these positions without coming to a general engagement, General Howe, on December 10, suddenly withdrew his army and marched back to 
2 CONC Philadelphia, where the troops went into winter quarters. December 11, General Washington took up his march with his army to Valley Forge, crossing the Schuylkill river at Swedes' Ford on the 12, and the army arrived at the Gulf M
2 CONC ills on December 13. Peter, Jacob and John Supplee were soldiers in the ranks of the American army during the campaigns herein recited, and Peter's teams were also used at that time in removing the army supplies from Whitmarsh to 
2 CONC Valley Forge.
2 CONT
2 CONT December 18 the army resumed its march from the Gulf Mills over the snow-covered roads to Valley Forge. That march, history informs us, could be traced by the blood stains upon the trodden snow left there by the shoeless feet of t
2 CONC he American soldiers. December 19, 1777, the American army went into winter quarters among the snowclad hills of Valley Forge. That whole winter there was a panorama of suffering, endurance and patriotism, and there upon the bende
2 CONC d knee Washington invoked the Divine assistance. On January 24, in that memorable cold winter of 1777-78, encamped with Washington's army upon the bleak hills of Valley Forge, surrounded by his compatriots in arms, in the darkest 
2 CONC days of the American Revolution, Peter Supplee, a martyr to liberty, gave his life for the liberty we now enjoy. After his death at Valley Forge, his remains were taken to the Bethel cemetery and there interred. After Peter Supple
2 CONC e had been dead one hundred and twenty-five years, his great-grandson, Edwin M. Suplee of Phoenixville, Pennsylvania, after discovering his resting place, conceived the project of erecting a monument at his grave in grateful remem
2 CONC brance of his services and great sacrifice to his country's cause, and after a year's labor amongst Peter Supplee's descendants succeeded in raising money enough to erect a monument.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 28, 1903, a monument was erected at Bethel Cemetery.  It bears the following inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT IN MEMORY OF PETER SUPPLEE, A REVOLUTIONARY SOLDIER, BORN SEPT. 2ND, 1745, ENLISTED SEPT. 12, 1777, DIED WHILST ENCAMPED AT VALLEY FORGE, JANUARY 24TH, 1778, A MARTYR TO LIBERTY.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Peter Supplee married Susanna Wagoner 6 Mar 1774. Peter enlisted in Cpt.Charles Wilson Peale's Company on 22 Sep 1777, the day after the Battle of Brandywine. He was involved in Battles at Germantown and Whitemarsh and retreated w
2 CONC ith the Continental Army to Valley Forge in Jan 1778. He sickened and died. His will was dated 20 Jan 1778. His wife Susanna Wagoner was born 4 Jun 1750 in Philadelphia County, PA. She died 31 Aug 1817 in Yates Co. NY. She is buri
2 CONC ed in Sheppard Lot, Pen Yan.Peter and Susannah had two children. Rachel born 18 Jan 1775 and Peter born 8 Feb 1778. Note: Died of camp fever at Valley Forge / husband of Susanna Wagener Supplee
2 CONT * Reference: [https://old.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=14677297 Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 26 2018, 1:52:10 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War Soldier. Private. Jacob, along with his brothers John and Peter belonged to Captain Charles Wilson Peale's company of Philadelphia Militia. That company belonged to the Fourth Battalion of the Philadelphia Regime
2 CONC nt of Foot.
