1 NAME Rebecca /Mayberry/
2 GIVN Rebecca
2 SURN Mayberry
2 _MARNM Prentiss
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1794
2 PLAC Belleville, Wood, West Virginia, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE MAR 1850
2 PLAC Adams, La Salle, Illinois, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca Mayberry (c. 1795–c. 1860s): Born circa 1795 in Virginia, Rebecca was the daughter of George Mayberry and Rebecca (Pennybacker) Mayberry. Her maternal family were prominent Shenandoah Valley ironmasters—her grandfather, Di
2 CONC rck Pennybacker, and father, George Mayberry, built and operated the Columbia Furnace and Redwell Furnace in Virginia before her branch of the family joined the early 19th-century migration west to Wood County along the Ohio River
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT She married on April 14, 1814, in Belleville, Wood County, Virginia to Henry Leonidas Prentiss (1788–1849).  He was the son of John Prentiss and Esther Richards of New London, Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Rebecca raised a family that achieved significant local and national prominence:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lucy (Prentiss) Bowles: Born in New London, Connecticut; settled in Adams County, Illinois, where she married pioneer farmer Augustin E. Bowles (1815–1872) as his second wife and raised seven children in Quincy.
2 CONT # Mary Catherine (Prentiss) Goodno: Born in Virginia (Wood County area); migrated westward with her parents through Missouri to Illinois, where she married into the Goodno family and resided in Quincy.
2 CONT # Maj. Gen. Benjamin Mayberry Prentiss (1819–1901): Born November 23, 1819, in Belleville, Virginia; worked in rope manufacturing in Quincy before rising to national renown as a Union Army Major General in the Civil War, famous fo
2 CONC r defending the "Hornet's Nest" at Shiloh (1862) and winning the Battle of Helena (1863).
2 CONT # Aurelia Prentiss (1821–1838): Born in Virginia; died as a teenager at age 17 in Marion County, Missouri, shortly after the family's westward migration out of Virginia and prior to their relocation to Quincy, Illinois.
2 CONT # Henry Clay Prentiss: Born during the family's Virginia/Missouri period; named in honor of statesman Henry Clay and worked alongside his father and brother Benjamin in cordage and rope manufacturing in Quincy.
2 CONT # Patrick Adair Prentiss: Born in Virginia during the family's residence in Wood County; recorded in early family registers alongside his brothers Benjamin and Henry Clay.
2 CONT # Henrietta Prentiss: Died in early childhood prior to the family's mid-1830s move west to Missouri and Illinois.
