1 OCCU Confederate officer, Capt. of the 9th Tennessee Cavalry under Gen. John H. Morgan, POW, President of the Bank of Hartsville
1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Descendants of William Edward Ellis:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~tnsumner/fawedellis.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Hudson C. Ellis was a Captain in the 9th Tennessee Cavalry under General John H. Morgan. He was captured on the Ohio raid and imprisoned at Columbus when his chief escaped from that prison. He was successful in business, owning a 
2 CONC large farm by the Cumberland River, and was president of the Bank of Hartsville from its organization, in 1884, until his death, which occurred in Hartsville in October, 1908. Hudson owned the area today known as "Ellis Square", a
2 CONC  few miles northeast of Nashville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain married Josephine Towson, born in Brazos, Texas, daughter of Jacob Towson, a wealthy family from Baltimore, Maryland and a leading citizen in Hartsville. She was an ardent Confederate Daughter. Josephine interviewed with P
2 CONC resident Lincoln in behalf of Captain Ellis which is rumored to have inspired the movie starring Shirley Temple, "The Littlest Rebel."
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From H. C. Ellis: Written by Jay Guy Cisco, From Historic Sumner County, Tennessee, 1909
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~tnsumner/sumnelli.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain H. C. Ellis was born in Sumner County, near Fountain Head, in 1818. He was related to the Gwin family, from which Senator William Gwin was descended. In 1843 he removed to Hartsville, where he engaged in merchandising unti
2 CONC l the beginning of the Civil War, when he entered the Confederate army as Captain of a company in the Ninth Tennessee Regiment of Cavalry under General John H. Morgan, with whom he served, and was captured on Morgan's Ohio raid, a
2 CONC nd was in prison with the officers of Morgan's command at Columbus, Ohio. From Columbus he was sent to Fort Delaware, where he was held prisoner until his exchange in 1864, when he rejoined his command and served until paroled in 
2 CONC North Carolina after the surrender of General Lee. As a citizen he was public spirited and was foremost in every enterprise for the good of his town and county. He was successful in business and accumulated a considerable fortune,
2 CONC  and was at his death on of the wealthiest men in Trousdale County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Ellis married Miss Josephine Towson, with whom he lived over half a century, and to whom he clung with a beautiful devotion until the last ray of reason was gone. Mrs. Ellis survives her husband and spends much of her time
2 CONC  in travel and in visiting her many friends and relatives, ever keeping in mind the sacred memory of her honored and noble husband. Captain Ellis was one of the founders of the first bank in Hartsville, and was its president from 
2 CONC its organization in 1884 to his death, October 7, 1903. Captain Ellis left no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note from Lloyd D. Ellis (August 28, 1999): The name of H. C. Ellis was Hudson Chamberlain Ellis. His date of death is incorrect. He died October 17, 1908 in the town of Hartsville, TN.
