1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From his obituary via Find A Grave
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33636378/edward-s-ellis
2 CONT
2 CONT The Springdale (AR) News of Friday, June 23, 1899
2 CONT
2 CONT Some eight weeks ago E.S. Ellis and wife came in from their home on White River to visit at the home of Dr. E.F. Ellis. Mr. Ellis was taken sick on the road and on arriving at the home of his son took to his bed and never left it 
2 CONC again. He was first afflicted with a kidney trouble and had every attention that devoted friends and skilled physicians could render but it was all of no avail and he passed away Monday morning at 4 o'clock, the immediate cause of
2 CONC  his death being blood poisoning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains were interred in the cemetery at Gearin's Chapel, three miles this side of Fayetteville, near the site of the old home of the deceased. The funeral was under the direction of the local Odd Fellow's Lodge, although a sh
2 CONC ort service was held at the residence by Rev. E.L. Massey, pastor of the South Methodist Church, and at the grave by Rev. N.M. Ragland, pastor of the First Christian Church of Fayetteville. The funeral procession was one of the la
2 CONC rgest ever seen in Springdale. 
2 CONT
2 CONT E.S. Ellis was born in Sumner County, Tenn. February 4, 1837. At the age of 9 years he went with his parents to Missouri, settling near what is now the town of Nevada. Here his boyhood days were spent, when he moved to St. Clair C
2 CONC ounty, Mo. At the breaking out of the civil war he espoused the cause of the South and enlisted in the army, serving his country faithfully until peace was declared. He was under Gen. Price. 
2 CONT
2 CONT February 16, 1861 he was united in marriage to Miss Martha J. Stearns. Returning to his devastated home at the close of the war he found the prejudice so strong that against those who had been favorable to the cause of the South t
2 CONC hat he decided to remove to Arkansas, locating on a farm three miles this side of Fayetteville. He remained here until 1872 when he purchased a fine farm northeast of town on White River where he resided until his death. As a resu
2 CONC lt of his marriage with Miss Stearns five sons were born to them, 
2 CONT *the oldest of which is Dr. E.F. Ellis of this city. 
2 CONT *Robert, who died at the age of three years; 
2 CONT *Will E., who resides near Vinita, I.T.; 
2 CONT *the next died while an infant; 
2 CONT *the last was Mark St. Clair Ellis, a graduate of the naval academy at Annapolis and who is now in the service with the rank of ensign. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Ellis died February 15, 1877 and in September 1882 Mr. Ellis was united in marriage to Miss Fannie Mullins, daughter of the late Judge Thomas Mullins. Mrs. Ellis survives her husband. From that marriage two children were born
2 CONC , 
2 CONT *a son, Tom, and 
2 CONT *a daughter, Jessie, the latter having died at three years of age. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Ellis was a member of the I.O.O.F. and also a member of the Christian Church although he had not taken an active interest in church work in late years. Such, in brief, in the career of Uncle Ned Ellis, than whom no truer or mo
2 CONC re honorable man lived. Of virtues he had many but if a single fault we never discovered. He was fair and honest in all his dealings with his fellowmen and in life he had the friendship and good will of all who knew him and in dea
2 CONC th they honor and revere his memory. The family of the deceased have the sincerest sympathy of every one in the community.
