1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mary J. Winifred Martin was married to Jesse Stoball McClure at the home of her parents, David & Hannah Martin, near Sampsell Station, Livingston County, Missouri on December 26, 1856.  The couple went in a covered
2 CONC  wagon across the then wild Indian Territory to Tarrant County, Texas and settled what was then about eight miles south of Ft. Worth.  They lived in the wagon until Jesse could cut the logs and build them a two room cabin.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Before Jesse Martin was born, Jesse Stoball had to go to the Civil War.  He was a private in the Seventh Regiment, Company K, Texas Mounted Volunteers.  In this entlistment he was in Sibley's New Mexico Campaign, seeing action at 
2 CONC Val Verde and Clorieta Pass.  After Sibley's command returned to Texas, Jesse Stoball McClure was assigned to the Kentucky Third and was attached to Morgan's Raiders.  He was captured near Cincinnati and put in prison in Chicago, 
2 CONC from which he escaped after eighteen months.  He made his way by foot back to his little family.
2 CONT
2 CONT (from member tree, Ancestry.com)
