1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT @R1203558009@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=153930756&pid=1063 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT @R1203558009@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=158403873&pid=1633 
2 CONT mother - father footstone  age 55 y 11 m 11 d
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Firestone had a first wife - Elizabeth Brinker or Brinkman and had children: 
2 CONT Susannah b 1819, 
2 CONT Barbara b 1822
2 CONT and Andrew b 1824 -  
2 CONT
2 CONT to date have not found Susannah or Andrew.
2 CONT
2 CONT added: s/o Nicholas Firestone and Elizabeth Shane
2 CONT
2 CONT Update Sept 26, 2008 by researcher P Firestone -
2 CONT Peter's b-day is 2 June 1797 in Maryland.
2 CONT His 2nd wife was born in 1808 in Ohio
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT To read more to to the following website with many photos:
2 CONT  Firestone Farm
2 CONT
2 CONT The Firestone Farm was originally built by Peter Firestone in 1828 in Columbiana, Ohio (just a few miles from the Pennsylvania border), and is now a gem among gems inside Greenfield Village. Among the family members living there i
2 CONC n the latter half of the 19th century was young Harvey Firestone, the grandson of Peter, who would later gain fame and fortune in the tire industry and became a close friend of Henry Ford.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 19th and into the 20th century, the Firestones raised a large flock of sheep, with wool being their 'cash crop,' but they also harvested oats, hay, corn, and wheat. In 1965, nearly thirty years after Harvey's death, his
2 CONC  descendants and the local historical society restored the house and opened it to the public for tours, but because of the farm's remote location, it failed to attract many visitors.
2 CONT In 1983, Harvey's two surviving sons, both in their 70's, gave the house and barn, together with furnishings and a sizable endowment for maintenance, to Greenfield Village as a way to keep the memory of their father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disassembling the buildings and reconstructing them some two hundred miles away took over two years. During the dis-assembly and reconstruction, however, the crew made a very interesting discovery: a note tucked beneath a staircas
2 CONC e, signed, dated, and hidden by none other than 14 year old Harvey himself, inadvertently revealed the date of the 1882 restoration!
2 CONT
2 CONT The Firestone Farm, as it stands now in Greenfield Village, is a living history re-creation of life on a farm of the 1880's in Eastern Ohio, and has been restored to look as it did in 1882, when Harvey's parents remodeled the hous
2 CONC e to give it a more modern look. The wallpaper and furnishings throughout the house show what was considered stylish during the Victorian era. The next four pictures show the 'best room' - the parlor - showing the phenomenal job t
2 CONC he curators did:* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/5223217/peter-firestone Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 27 2022, 5:32:45 UTC''
