1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''David Wagoner'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/39167778/david-wagener
2 CONT
2 CONT Pioneer. In 1791 he opened the first public house in Yates County. Later Wagener bought a sawmill at the site of modern Penn Yan; then he built a gristmill and arranged for what is now Main Street to be surveyed through the woods 
2 CONC to his mill. He attracted a physician and other settlers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His estate included much of present day Penn Yan's commercial area. His older son, Abraham, was the first postmaster and then the first mayor of Penn Yan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wagener was a disciple of "the Public Universal Friend," Jemima Wilkinson. His burial in 1799 was the first in Lakeview Cemetery. Lakeview is owned by the village of Penn Yan and on the National Register of Historic Places.
