2 CONT Samuel Emery's Will
2 CONT 3em>I Samuel Emery Sen. of the county of Highland and State  of Ohio of lawful age of sound mind and memory do make and ordain this my last will and testament.
2 CONT 3em>1st I do give & bequeath to my wife Sarah and my daughter Rebecca and Sarah all my household furniture beds & bedding clothing books and kitchen furniture for their own use and benefit
2 CONT 3em>2nd I give and bequeath too my wife Sarah all the residue of my personal property of every kind after paying all my lawfull debts & funeral expenses to be used for her benefit during her natural life time.
2 CONT 3em>3d I give and bequeath all my personal property that may remain after the death of my wife Sarah to be equally divided between all of my children or their heirs.
2 CONT 3em>4th I give and bequeath tp my son Richard one acre of land to be as laid off as to include the house and other out buildings and garden now occupied by him.
2 CONT 3em>5th I give and bequeath to my wife Sarah all my real estate to be occupied by her and used for her benefit during her natural life time.
2 CONT 3em>6th I give and bequeath (after the death of my wife Sarah) all my land to be equally divided between my following named children towit: Samuel, Richard, Sarah, Issabel, Elizabeth, Clementine and Polly of their legal heirs.
2 CONT 3em>7th I do further bequeath that in the division of my land it be so divided as to include in the part set off to my son Richard the one acre above bequeathed to him making his equal with the others including the one acre
2 CONT 3em>8th I do further bequeath that if my daughter Rebecca should remain single after the death of my wife Sarah my land be so divided as to include in the part assigned to my daughter Rebecca the house i now occupy
2 CONT 3em>In testimony I have hereunto set my hand and seal this 18th day of March A.D. 1856 Samuel Emery
2 CONT 3em>Attest Johm M. Banere, Philip Gibler, Gideon Jackson
2 CONT 3em>The State of Ohio Highland county SS Highland Probate Court April 17th A.D. 1856 This day the last will and testament of Samuel Emery late of Highland county, Ohio deceased was produced in open Court where upon came personally
2 CONC  John M. Banere , Philip Gibler and Gideon Jackson subscribing witnesses thereto who being first fully sworn depose  and say that said Samuel Emery at the time of executing and publishing said last will and testament (which is her
2 CONC eto attached) was over the age of twenty-one years of sound mind and memory and not under any restraint that he voluntarily subscribed  said last will and testament and in their presence and hearing [published and declared the sam
2 CONC e to ne his last will and testament and that they in his presence and at his request signed the same as subscribing witnesses thereto and in the presence of each other. John N. Banere, Philip Gibler Gideon Jackson Sworn to and sub
2 CONC scribed in open court this 17th day of April A. D. 1856
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rev. Rufus Emery, Genealogical Records of Descendants of John and Anthony Emery of Newbury, Mass. 1590 - 1890, The  (1890, Salem Publishing and Printing Co. The Salem Press, 1891), pg. 51. 
2 CONT 3em>son of Ambrose and his second wife Rebecca (Yocum) Emery; married Sarah Anderson, daughter of Richard and Mary Anderson, Cumberland Township, Greene co., Pa., April 7, 1808 (b. Greene co., Pa., 1791); loved in 1815 in Greene c
2 CONC o., Pa., then near New Market, O., where he died April 5, 1856. His wife died April 6, 1881, in New Market. Children Listed: Clementine, Ambrose, Mary, Rebecca, Isabel, John, Elizabeth, Samuel Jr., Sarah, Nancy, Richard Anderson a
2 CONC nd James M.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith Elaine Burns, Revised Genealogical Records of Descendants of John Emery of Newbury, Massachusetts,The (1982, Gateway Press Inc. Baltimore, Md.), pg. 90.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Records of Highland co., Oh. 1805-1880 by David N. McBride & Jane N. McBride;  
2 CONT
2 CONT Cemtery Inscriptons of Highland co., Oh. by David and Jane McBride pg. 386
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Ambrose Emery, was a participant in the Boston Tea Party and was recognized by the DAR for providing soldiers with food and supplies at the Battle of Brandywine.
2 CONT Samuel was a farmer, and the 8 July 1832 issue of The Hillsborough Gazette recorded that Samuel gave the following volunteer toast at the 4th of July Celebration: "Andrew Jackson, his excellency; may he preside with honor in the p
2 CONC residential chair."
2 CONT Samuel and his wife, Sarah Anderson were married on 7 April 1808 in Cumberland Twp., Greene Co., PA.
2 CONT His son, Richard Anderson Emery married Mary Ann Larrick.
