2 CONT
2 CONT Married: (2) Johann (Jan) Strepers (Streypers) 5/12/1669, Kaldenkirchen Reformed church.  he bought 5,000 acres William Penn for 100 pounds sterling, as much land as Jacob Telner and Dirk Shipman.  Jan and Anna did not emigrate to
2 CONC  America but possibly spend a short time there.  Entgen/Anna d Kaldenkirchen 08/02/1700, Jan Strypers k Kaldendirchen on 05/06/1715.  Jan and his brother, Wilhem, Streypers, were son of a merchant  of Kaldendirchen and the two boy
2 CONC s jointly inherited his business; when Wilhem left for America the estate had not been divided.  Jan became an investor and was one of the land purchasers for the Krefelders in PA.  Jan did not emigrate to America.  Jan sent his b
2 CONC rother, Wilhem, to settle the land.  He became sole owner of the family's estate in Kaldendirchen, and as compensation he turned over all his land holdings in America  to his brother, Wilhem.  Jan's three children all died in Germ
2 CONC any.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT (1)-Lenart/ Leonard Kurlis among Krefeld voyage in 1683, age 13; he was placed in care of Jan and Merchken Lensen in exchange for 50 acres of land and use of a linen weaving stool and three combs for three years.  He was to be tau
2 CONC ght the weaving trade.  They were the only emigrants of 1683 to remain Mennonites after arrival in Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2)-Metgen/ Mechtild born on March 9, 1664, married (1) Henderjik Jansen Van Acken/Aken.  The same Hendrick Jans who with Herman Isacks op den Graff published the pamphlet on their persecutions in Holland.  Lived in Krefeld from 1
2 CONC 675.  Did not emigrant to PA.  Probably buried as a Krefeld  townsman on December 14,1696.  He must have been a son of Mennonite Jan Claussen ( Van Acken ans his wife, Dreutgen, who was expelled from Gladach in 1654, he later live
2 CONC d in Rheydt, much have endured the persecution and imprisonment.  They had the following children.
