1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/33420487/jacob-isaacs-van_bebber
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Van_Bibber-43
2 CONT NOTE: The birth date of 1630 below is wrong.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Jacob Isaacs VAN BEBBER==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Isaac VAN BEBBER and Hester OP DEN GRAEFF
2 CONT * Born around  1630 in Krefeld (Germany) Holland; 
2 CONT * occupation: Baker
2 CONT * Death:  Sep 1705 in Cecil Co, MD. He was the 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Spouse==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married p: Christina Unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children of Jacob VAN BEBBER and Christina are:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Deborah van Bebber, died 1739 in Bohemia Manor, MD; married Herman Isaacs Op den GRAEFF May 07, 1691 in Germantown, PA. 
2 CONT * Matthias van Bebber. 
2 CONT * Lisbit Van Bebber, married Herman Isaacs Op den GRAEFF. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 11th of June, 1683, Penn conveyed to Govert Remke, Lenart Arets, and Jacob Isaacs Van Bebber, a baker, all of Crefeld, one thousand acres of land each, and they, together with Telner, Streypers, and Sipman, constituted the 
2 CONC original Crefeld purchasers. It is evident that their purpose was colonization, and not speculation. The arrangement between Penn and Sipman provided that a certain number of families should go to Pennsylvania within a specified t
2 CONC ime, and probably the other purchasers entered into similar stipulations.[9] However that may be, ere long thirteen men with their families, comprising thirty-three persons, nearly all of whom were relatives, were ready to embark 
2 CONC to seek new homes across the ocean. They were Lenart Arets, Abraham Op den Graeff, Dirck Op den Graeff, Hermann Op den Graeff, Willem Streypers, Thones Kunders, Reynier Tyson, Jan Seimens, Jan Lensen, Peter Keurlis, Johannes Bleik
2 CONC ers, Jan Lucken, and Abraham Tunes. The three Op den Graeffs were brothers, Hermann was a son-in-law of Van Bebber, they were accompanied by their sister Margaretha, and they were cousins of Jan and Willem Streypers, who were also
2 CONC  brothers. The wives of Thones Kunders and Lenart Arets were sisters of the Streypers, and the wife of Jan was the sister of Reynier Tyson. Peter Keurlis was also a near relative, and the location of the signatures of Jan Lucken a
2 CONC nd Abraham Tunes on the certificate of the marriage of a son of Thones Kunders with a daughter of Willem Streypers in 1710 indicates that they too were connected with the group by family ties.[10] On the 7th of June, 1683, Jan Str
2 CONC eypers and Jan Lensen entered into an agreement at Crefeld by the terms of which Streypers was to let Lensen have fifty acres of land at a rent of a rix dollar and half a stuyver, and to lend him fifty rix dollars for eight years 
2 CONC at the interest of six rix dollars annually. Lensen was to transport himself and wife to Pennsylvania, to clear eight acres of Streyper's land and to work for him twelve days in each year for eight years. The agreement proceeds, “
2 CONC I further promise to lend him a Linnen-weaving stool with 3 combs, and he shall have said weaving stool for two years . . and for this Jan Lensen shall teach my son Leonard in one year the art of weaving, and Leonard shall be boun
2 CONC d to weave faithfully during said year.” On the 18th of June the little colony were in Rotterdam, whither they were accompanied by Jacob Telner, Dirck Sipman, and Jan Streypers, and there many of their business arrangements were c
2 CONC ompleted. Telner conveyed 2000 acres of land to the brothers Op den Graeff, and Sipman made Hermann Op den Graeff his attorney. Jan Streypers conveyed 100 acres to his brother Willem, and to Seimens and Keurlis each 200 acres. Ble
2 CONC ikers and Lucken each bought 200 acres from Benjamin Furly, agent for the purchasers at Frankfort. At this time James Claypoole, a Quaker merchant in London, who had previously had business relations of some kind with Telner, was 
2 CONC about to remove with his family to Pennsylvania, intending to sail in the Concord, Wm. Jeffries, master, a vessel of 500 tons burthen. Through him a passage from London was engaged for them in the same vessel, which was expected t
2 CONC o leave Gravesend on the 6th of July, and the money was paid in advance.[11] It is now ascertained definitely that eleven of these thirteen emigrants were from Crefeld, and the presumption that their two companions, Jan Lucken and
2 CONC  Abraham Tunes, came from the same city is consequently strong. This presumption is increased by the indications of relationship, and the fact that the wife of Jan Seimens was Mercken Williamsen Lucken. Fortunately, however, we ar
2 CONC e not wanting in evidence of a general character. Pastorius,[12] after having an interview with Telner at Rotterdam a few weeks earlier, accompanied by four servants, who seem to have been Jacob Schumacher, Isaac Dilbeeck, George 
2 CONC Wertmuller, and Koenradt Rutters, had gone to America representing both the purchasers at Frankfort and Crefeld. In his references to the places at which he stopped on his journey down the Rhine he nowhere mentions emigrants excep
2 CONC t at Crefeld, where he says: “I talked with Tunes Kunders and his wife, Dirck, Hermann, and Abraham Op den Graeff and many others, who six weeks later followed me.”[13] For some reason the emigrants were delayed between Rotterdam 
2 CONC and London, and Claypoole was in great uneasiness for fear the vessel should be compelled to sail without them, and they should lose their passage money. He wrote several letters about them to Benjamin Furly at Rotterdam. June 19t
2 CONC h he says, “I am glad to hear the Crevill friends are coming.” July 3d he says, “before I goe away wch now is like to be longer than we expected by reason of the Crevill friends not coming we are fain to loyter and keep the ship s
2 CONC till at Black wall upon one pretence or another;” and July 10th he says, " “It troubles me much that the friends from Crevillt are not yet come.”[14] As he had the names of the thirty-three persons, this contemporary evidence is v
2 CONC ery strong, and it would seem safe to conclude that all of this pioneer band, which, with Pastorius, founded Germantown, came from Crefeld. Henry Melchior Muhlenberg says the first comers were platt-deutch from the neighborhood of
2 CONC  Cleves.[15] Despite the forebodings of Claypoole the emigrants reached London in time for the Concord, and they set sail westward on the 24th of July. While they are for the first time experiencing the dangers and trials of a voy
2 CONC age across the ocean, doubtless sometimes looking back with regret, but oftener wistfully and wonderingly forward, let us return to inquire who these people were who were willing to abandon forever the old homes and old friends al
2 CONC ong the Rhine, and commence new lives with the wolf and the savage in the forests upon the shores of the Delaware.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' - https://en.m.wikisource.org/wiki/Historical_and_biographical_sketches/01_Settlement_of_Germantown
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes for Jacob Isaacs VAN BEBBER:==
2 CONT
2 CONT One of William Penn's original six purchasers in Germantown, Jacob Isaacs van Bebber was called a baker of Krefeld. Although he opened his home for Mennonite church services -- he was one of the first Mennonites to arrive -- some 
2 CONC historians question his inclusion on the 1708 membership list of the Germantown Mennonite Church. He eventually moved to High Street, which evidently suggests he wasn't Mennonite. (His inclusion is also questionable since at least
2 CONC  one source gives his death as 1705.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Isaacs left his tracks in early Germantown records: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1683 - as a first purchaser, receives 1000 acres on June 11. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1684 - arrives in Germantown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT receives another 161 acres in the Germantown land distribution on May 2. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1689 - receives more land in the distribution 
2 CONT
2 CONT appointed one of the first committeemen by Penn 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1691 - named a burgess 
2 CONT
2 CONT naturalized, along with 63 others 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1692 - Sept. 29 "Jacob Isaacs and Cornelius Sojerts delivered unto each other a Deed of Exchange of 
2 CONT
2 CONT land viz 50 acres in the Germantownship." 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1693 - signed testimony for George Keith 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1694 - cited for insufficient fences on Sept. 13 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1695 - excused from appearing in court because he was in Philadelphia 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1698 - received land "on a good street in the city of Philadelphia and one third of an acre and...on the city ground 10 acres." 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704 - attorney Pastorius delivered a deed of sale for 61 acres in Crefeld (a village in Germantownship north of Germantown) to Thomas Rutter and Samuel Savage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Ulle, "Research Notes: Materials on Mennonites in Colonial Germantown," THE MENNONITE QUARTERLY REVIEW, Vol LVII, No 4, October 1983, pp 355-56: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a. Samuel Pennypacker, THE SETTLEMENT OF GERMANTOWN (Philadelphia: W.J. Campbell,       1899), pp 33, 124, 134, 142, 217 
2 CONT
2 CONT b. GURND UND LAGER BUCH, volume kept by Frances Daniel Pastorius, Recorder of Deeds Office, City of Philadelphia 
2 CONT
2 CONT c. William Hull, WILLIAM PENN & THE DUTCH QUAKER MIGRATION TO PENNSYLVANIA, (Swarthmore, 1935), p 187. 
2 CONT ----    
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Isaacs van Bebber, a member of the Krefeld Mennonite Church, acquired 1,000 acres of land from William Penn through Benjamin Furly on 11 June 1683, and was one of the first six Krefeld land buyers in America. He was temporar
2 CONC ily the agent of Dirk Sipman of Krefeld, who had on 10 March 1682, bought 5,000 acres of Penn, but did not emigrate. He came to Germantown in 1687, moved to Philadelphia before 1698. He died September 1705 at Earlsville, Cecil Cou
2 CONC nty, Maryland. He was the father of Isaac Jacobs van Bebber and Matthias Jacobs van Bebber.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassel, Daniel K. Geschichte der Mennoniten: von Menno Simons’ Austritt aus der Römisch-Katholischen Kirche in 1536 bis zu deren Auswanderung nach Amerika in 1683 ; mehr speciell ihre Ansiedlung und Ausbreitung in Amerika. Enthalt
2 CONC end: kurze Skizzen der einzelnen Gemeinden mit den Namen ihrer Prediger vom Jahre 1683 bis zur gegenwärtigen Zeit. Philadelphia: J. Kohler, 1890: 37, 41, 77.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hege, Christian and Christian Neff. Mennonitisches Lexikon, 4 vols. Frankfurt & Weierhof: Hege; Karlsruhe; Schneider, 1913-1967: v. I, 148.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hull, William Isaac. William Penn and the Dutch Quaker Migrations to Pennsylvania. Swarthmore, PA, Swarthmore College, c1935: 187, 215, 243, 253 f., 397, 403, 417.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jacob Isaac Van Bebber." RootsWeb.Com. Accessed 5 December 2006. <http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~jboales/d0006/g0000666.html#I151> 
2 CONT
2 CONT  b. abt. 1640 Krefeld, Nordhein-Westfalen, GERMANY 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT acob Isaac VAN BEBBER
2 CONT
2 CONT [74]
2 CONT
2 CONT CA. 1640 - Sep 1705
2 CONT
2 CONT     * OCCUPATION: Krefeld, Holland (Germany)
2 CONT
2 CONT       BAKER
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PROPERTY: 1000 acres from Wm. Penn (6 original buyers)
2 CONT
2 CONT       11 Jun 1683
2 CONT
2 CONT     * PROPERTY: founded Bebber Township (later called Skippack in Montgomery County)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * BIRTH: CA. 1640, Krefeld, Holland (Germany)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * IMMIGRATION: 1687, Germantown(Philadelphia), PA
2 CONT
2 CONT     * EVENT: 31 May 1691, Germantown charter of incorporation as burgess
2 CONT
2 CONT     * DEATH: Sep 1705, Earlsville, Cecil Co, MD
2 CONT
2 CONT     * BURIAL: in the floor of St. Stephen's Church, Earlsville, MD 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Isaacs Van Bibber (son of Isaac Van Bibber and Hester Op den Graeff) was born Abt. 1630 in Krefeld, Holland (Germany)1670, and died September 1705 in Cecil Co., MD1670. He married Christina Hermania on Abt. 1660 in Krefeld, 
2 CONC Germany1670.
2 CONT
2 CONT Includes NotesNotes for Jacob Isaacs Van Bibber: From Kenneth Smith:
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY OF JACOB ISAACS VANBIBBER
2 CONT
2 CONT The VanBibber family originated in Holland. The name Bebber is derived from the village of Bebber in the Dutchy of Cleves. Jacob Isaacs was born about 1640, probably in Krefeld, a city in the lower Rhine within a few miles of Holl
2 CONC and. Around 1660, Jacob married Christina Hermana, probably in Krefeld. The Van Bibbers were Mennonites, a religious sect that was persecuted at the time in Europe. At the invitation of William Penn, a number of Mennonites emigrat
2 CONC ed from Holland and the Palatinate and settled in Pennsylvania around 1683. Penn conveyed 1000 acres of land to each of the following men: Govert Remke, Lenarts Arets and Jacob Isaacs VanBibber. The three, along with three other m
2 CONC en, Telmer, Streypers and Sipman, were the original purchasers of the land on which Germantown was founded. The people who settled there were true settlers, having been treated harshly in Germany for many years because of their re
2 CONC ligion. Telmer and VanBibber were known to be Mennonites.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob VanBibber's son Isaac Jacobs, of Crefeld, came to America before his father. He was in the Germantown area in 1684. Jacob, his wife and another son, Matthias, came to Pennsylvania in 1687. Jacob and Christina settled in Germ
2 CONC antown, near Philadelphia and Matthias moved to the Skipeck area. Jacob Isaac moved to Philadelphia before 1698, where he was a merchant on High Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Starting in 1702, Matthias bought land in Cecil County, Maryland. By 1714, he owned the large estate known as Bohemia Manor in Cecil County, along with other estates. Brothers Isaac and Hendrick moved to cecil County also. The Van
2 CONC Bibber family resided in Cecil County for years. Jacob and Christina must have moved to Maryland also, because the records of St. Stephen's parish, Cecil County, Maryland say that Jacob Isaac VanBibber was buried there in Septembe
2 CONC r, 1705, and his wife Christina was buried there on September 4, 1711.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from "The Van Bibber family" by Josephine Letts in the Van Bibber Newsletter, May 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT More About Jacob Isaacs Van Bibber: Burial: September 02, 1705, St. Stephen's Church, Erlsville, Maryland. Record Change: October 27, 20001670
2 CONT
2 CONT More About Jacob Isaacs Van Bibber and Christina Hermania: Marriage: Abt. 1660, Krefeld, Germany.1670
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Jacob Isaacs Van Bibber and Christina Hermania are:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. +Matthias Jacobs Van Bibber, b. Abt. 1663, d. Abt. 1739, Cecil Co., MD1670.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT -------------------- Jacob Isaac Van Bibber  •Sex: M  •Birth: ABT 1630 in Krefeld, Germany  •Death: 1 SEP 1705 in Cecil Co. Md.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Isaac Van Bibber b: ABT 1610  Mother: Hester Opdengraeff b: 5 JAN 1609
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 1 Christina b: ABT 1640 in Krefeld, Germany •Married: ABT 1657
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 1. Lisbet Isaacs Van Bibber b: ABT 1657 in Krefeld, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Margaryte Van Bibber b: ABT 1658
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Deborah Van Bibber b: ABT 1660
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Isaacs Jacob Van Bibber b: ABT 1661 in Krefeld, Holland
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Matthias Jacobs Van Bibber b: ABT 1663 in Utrech, Holland
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Hendrick Jacobs Van Bibber b: ABT 1667 in Utrech, Holland
2 CONT ================================
2 CONT
2 CONT The VanBibber family originated in Holland. The name Bebber or Bibber is derived from the village of Bebber in the Dutchy of Cleves, which is now in Germany. Jacob Isaacs was born about 1640, probably in Krefeld, a city on the low
2 CONC er Rhine within a few miles of Holland. Around 1660, Jacob married Christina Hermana, probably in Krefeld. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The VanBibbers were Mennonites, a religious sect that was persecuted in Europe at the time. At the invitation of William Penn, a number of Mennonites emigrated from Holland and the Palatinate and settled in Pennsylvania around 168
2 CONC 3. Penn conveyed 1000 acres of land to each of the following men: Govert Remke, Lenarts Arets and Jacob Isaacs VanBibber. These three, along with three other men, Telner, Streypers and Sipman, were the original purchasers of the l
2 CONC and on which Germantown, which is now a suburb of Philadelphia, was founded. The people who settled there were true settlers, having been treated harshly in Germany for many years because of their religion. Telner and VanBibber we
2 CONC re known to be Mennonites. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob VanBibber's son Isaac Jacobs, of Crefeld, came to America before his father. He was in the Germantown area in 1684. Jacob, his wife and another son, Matthias, arrived in Pennsylvania in 1687. Jacob and Christina settled in G
2 CONC ermantown, near Philadelphia. The charter of incorporation for Germantown, dated May 31, 1691, names Jacob as a committeeman. Jacob was the first Recorder (city clerk?) in 1691 and a Burgess in 1692. Jacob moved to Philadelphia be
2 CONC fore 1698, where he was a merchant on High Street. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Starting in 1702, Matthias and his brothers Isaac and Hendrick bought land in Cecil County, Maryland because of a religious dispute in Germantown. By 1714, Matthias owned the large estate known as Bohemia Manor in Cecil County, al
2 CONC ong with other estates. Jacob and Christina moved to Maryland also, because the records of St. Stephen's parish, Cecil County, Maryland say that Jacob Isaac VanBibber was buried there in September, 1705, and his wife Christina was
2 CONC  buried there on September 4, 1711. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thanks to Ken Smith for the Bio. 
2 CONT ==================================
2 CONT
2 CONT From my families research they have that he was buried the floor of St. Stephen's Church in Earlsville, Maryland
2 CONT
2 CONT Shannon Wells
