1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.fultonhistory.org/pages/CEM-ALL.TXT Cemetery Information]: SIPES, George SILOAM b-1759; d-21 August 1843
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Henry A Sipes , Sr b: ABT 1734 in New Holland, Lancaster County Pennsylvania Mother: Mary Adams b: ABT 1735
2 CONT
2 CONT George Sipes married Catherine Kline who was born about  1762 in Maryland. They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Sipes b: 9 NOV 1789 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA Conrad Sipes b: 29 OCT 1791 in Bedford (now Fulton ) Co, PA Henry Sipes b: 12 NOV 1792 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA Mary 'Polly' Sipes b: 2 OCT 1796 in Bedford (now Fulton)
2 CONC  Co, PA John P. Sipes b: 4 MAR 1798 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA Jacob Ridge Sipes b: 2 AUG 1800 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA George Sipes , Jr b: 20 FEB 1806 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA James Sipes b: 1809 in Bedford Co (n
2 CONC ow Fulton), PA Rachel Sipes b: 1811 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA Mahala Catherine Sipes b: 1814 in Bedford (now Fulton) Co, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT He later married a Mary Magdalene.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Will:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen. I, George Sipes, Senr., of Licking Creek Township, Bedford County and State of Pennsylvania, being of perfect mind and memory thanks be to Almighty God, therefore calling unto mind the mortality of my bod
2 CONC y and knowing that it is apointed to all men to die do make and ordain this my last will and testament, that is to say principally and first of all I give my soul into the hands of Almighty God that gave it and my body to the dust
2 CONC  from whence it came to be burried in a Christian like manner at the discretion of my executors nothing doubting at the general resurrection I shall receive the same agin by the power of Almighty God. And as touching such worldly 
2 CONC estate wherewith it pleased Almighty God to bestow upon me in this life, I bequeath demise and dispose of the same in the following manner, viz.: Ist. I gave and bequeath to my beloved wife Mary Magdaline the small house atached t
2 CONC o the big house said house to be put in good order garden and stable and necessary firewood, as long as she herself occupies said house and the use of the stove, also use of fruit what she needs, with all the household and kitchen
2 CONC  furniture, likewise two cows, if so many six sheep and six hogs if that many. Also three tons of hay, sixteen bushels of wheat, sixteen bushels of other grain all hay and grain to be delivered to her at the aforesaid house by eac
2 CONC h of my children, an equal share of said hay and grain annually, my daughter Catharine, deceased, her children accepted hir cows to run on pasture only when other chattels pastures on said place. All the above privileges and prope
2 CONC rty she only to while she remains my widow. As to my real property. First, I bequeath to my oldest daughter Anna that part of the improvement where George Hoop now resides on. Second. To my first son Conrad that part he improved. 
2 CONC Third. To my second son Henry that part of improvement he formerly resided on. Fourth. To my second daughter Mary one hundred and fifty acres of land including the improvements she now resides on, not to run higher up then the sma
2 CONC ll run at the north corner of the fence at the road, then up said run along the fence. Fifth. To my third son John the mansion place. Sixth. To my fourth son Jacob that part of improvement he now resides on. Seventh. To my son Hen
2 CONC ry one hundred and fifty acres of land being part of the same tract Richard Pittman now resides on, beginning at the corner before mentioned and up the old school house my son Henry to pay my two granddaughters Sara James and Cath
2 CONC arine Sipe James each seventy five dollars as when the become of age their share in full if the- live so long. If not said amount to be equally divided among all my heirs. Eighth. To my fifth son, George that part where the sawmil
2 CONC l stands on. Ninth. To my sixth son, James, the improvement in Providence Township, County and state aforesaid. The land to be divided among my heirs equal acres each as near can be, except my daughter Mary's and the hundred and f
2 CONC ifty acres which my son Henry is to get of the before mentioned tract, my son James to pay one dollar and fifty cents per acre, for all the acres, he has more than his equal share he is to pay them that may lack. And as to my pers
2 CONC onal property after my death and funeral expenses is paid to be divided among all my heirs share and share alike and further I do ordain and appoint my sons Conrad Sipe and Henry Sipe my executors of this my last will and testamen
2 CONC t in the year of our Lord one thousand eight hundred and forty, the sixth day of April, A. D. 1840.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, sealed and delivered in the presence of John F. Davis Joshua Pittman
2 CONT
2 CONT George Sipe (Seal)
2 CONT
2 CONT Be it remembered that on the 30th day of August, Anno Domini 1843, letters testamentary were issued to Conrad Sipe and Henry Sipe, executors in the foregoing will named they having been first duly sworn according to law. Jos. B. N
2 CONC oble, Register.
2 CONT
2 CONT State of Pennsylvania, County of Bedford, SS:
2 CONT
2 CONT I, Ella M. Stewart, Register for the probate of wills, etc., in and for said county, hereby certify that the foregoing copy of the last will and testament of George Sipe, late of "Licking Creek" township, (formerly in Bedford Coun
2 CONC ty) deceased, is true and correct, so full perfect and entire as the same is found on file, and of record in the Register of Wills office of Bedford County, Pennsylvania in Will Book No. 3, page 257, etc. In testimony whereof, I h
2 CONC ave hereunto set my hand and affixed the official seal of the said Register's Office, this 16th day of March, A. D., 1927
2 CONT
2 CONT Ella M. Stewart, Register of Wills, etc.
