1 NAME Clarissa Amanda /Masters/
2 GIVN Clarissa Amanda
2 SURN Masters
2 _MARNM Clarke
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Pistol River Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Pistol River
3 STAE Oregon
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1839
2 ADDR
3 STAE Ohio
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1912
2 ADDR
3 STAE Oregon
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Married Nathaniel Dehaven Clarke August 24, 1873, in San Joaquin County, California
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Clarissa (Clara) Clarke was born in Ohio and orphaned at 15.  She went west with strangers and was essentially abused and pretended to be married to an older man who protected her....sort of.  They ended up in San 
2 CONC Francisco where she worked as a midwife.  A younger man who passing through where Clara and her pretend husband lived in Colusa, California, impregnated her, but she stayed with the older, pretend husband.  Then she met Nathaniel 
2 CONC Clarke, who made violins, and they married and moved to Oregon.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara and Nathaniel's daughter, Irma was a gifted musician and artist.  She married Delmer Colegrove, Sr. about 1900 and they had 4 children, but sadly, Irma died in 1916 when her youngest child was only about 8 years old.  Delmer
2 CONC  later remarried to a younger woman named Sophia, who was not well liked by Delmer and Irma's children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Delmer was the son of Francis and Salina Colegrove, and he had a brother named Lafayette, but their parents split up in Arcata, California, and the mother kept Lafayette and the father kept Delmer, who was 5 years old.  They went 
2 CONC north looking for work up the Oregon Coast and stopped overnight at the Mountain Ranch, a 14-room ranch house owned by Raleigh and Janetta "Nettie" Scott.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Scott had only one daughter who died as a baby, and Mrs. Scott was lonely, and wanted to adopt Delmer.  Since Delmer wanted to stay at the ranch, his father went north without him and was killed about a year later in 
2 CONC a logging accident, so Delmer became the adopted son of Raleigh and Nettie Scott.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raleigh Scott was one of the most admired men of the region.  Unschooled, but apparently brilliant and well read, he had beautiful handwriting and gradually bought out the homestead properties of all his neighbors to create a 9,00
2 CONC 0 acre empire.  The Scott's considered Delmer their actual son, and they were grandpa and grandma Scott to Irma and Delmer's children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Delmer and Irma took their 4 children to the San Francisco World's Fair in 1915.  On the way to Sacramento they met up with Delmer's biological mother.  It was the first time they had seen each other in all those years.  Delmer's 
2 CONC brother, Lafayette, had visited Delmer at one point, but he too was killed in an accident at a fairly young age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidently Raleigh Scott and his wife Nettie were quite a positive influence on Delmer's life, taking in this abandoned child and putting him in charge of a large ranch operation, enabling him to be one of the first to own a car an
2 CONC d go to the World's Fair.  Delmer was an incredible worker according to everyone.  So was Raleigh Scott, who was extremely generous and helpful to all his neighbors everywhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Delmer and Irma's eldest daughter, Nettie, was obviously named for Mrs. Scott.  They had 2 sons, Delmer, Jr., and Stanley, who both married and had families.  Their youngest daughter, Nellie June, got married when she was 19 years
2 CONC  old and 9 months later she died in Grants Pass, Oregon.  The cause of her death is unknown, but perhaps it was due to childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The above information was provided by Kate Fitzgerald who was searching for details on her long-lost neighbors and found information I had posted about the Scott family on the Geni.com website.  My great grandfather's sister, Nanc
2 CONC y Malinda Rollins, was married to James Eugene Scott, who was the youngest brother of Raleigh Scott.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kate's family had moved to the Scott homestead ranch in 1969, and she felt like they were really more like her own relatives, so that is why she reached out to me about this family.  She also found a great deal of information abou
2 CONC t the family from a book entitled "Pistol River Memories," with interviews by Ella Spaunagle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the information from that book included the following about Raleigh Scott:  "Raleigh Scott, 1851-1920, came to Del Norte County, California, at the age of 15 years old with his brother Robert to visit their two uncles, Fel
2 CONC ix and Marion Scott.  He married Nettie Cooley at her family's home at the Chetco, Oregon, river and they went to homestead in the hills 14 miles north of her family's home." 
2 CONT
2 CONT This land was purchased by Liz's family in 1969, where they became neighbors for 50 years with the Scott's and Colegrove's descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Della Dale Smith-Pistelli, August 31, 2018.
