1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Leverett W. Olin, born February 12th, 185 1, in Streetsboro, Portage County, Ohio, where he spent a  few years of his life; his parents then moved into Franklin, onto the Grandfather Clark's old homestead, where he
2 CONC  remained with his father until his death, then assisted his mother for a time in carrying on the farm. He received the foundation of his education at Earlville district school, where so many Olin children attended at one time or 
2 CONC another. In 1869 he attended one term at Mt. Union College. In 1872 he began a course at Buchtel College, at Akron, Ohio, and atter somewhat more than a year's study was thrown behind his class by sickness and finally did not retu
2 CONC rn to resume his studies there. He then taught three terms of school in Streetsboro, after which he began the study of medicine with Dr. E. W. Price of Kent; attended his first term of lectures at Medical Department of Western Res
2 CONC erve College in Cleveland, Ohio, in the winters of 1876-'77- His second and third terms of lectures were received at the College of Physicians and Surgeons, New York City, by which he was graduated, March 12th, 1880. After receivi
2 CONC ng his degree of M. D., he returned home and after a short stay went into Indiana, where finally located at Elmdale, a little " burg " in Coalcreek township, Montgomery county, and commenced the practice of medicine. With pardonab
2 CONC le pride, he gives the name of his first patient, and declares that he is yet " still living." He still resides and practices his profession in the same place where he first located. He was married September 2nd, 1883, in the parl
2 CONC ors of the Nutt House, in Crawfordsville, Indiana, to Effie Swank, daughter of Benjamin and Mary E. Swank. She was born in Coalcreek township, Montgomery County, Indiana, on January 13th, 1863. She was one of a family of ten child
2 CONC ren, and was brought up and always lived in the township of her birth. Her father at first carried on the farm, which he still owns, but after a time began the study of law, and has since adopted that profession, which he is follo
2 CONC wing. Her education was obtained at the common schools, known as the Oakland School.  They have had four children: Lester Weir Olin,  born September 23rd, 1884, in Elmdale, Montgomery  county, Ind.; Blanche Olin, born in Elmdale, 
2 CONC Ind.,  July 4th, 1886; Edna Ray Olin, born in Elmdale,  January 28th, 1889, died March 8th, 1889; buried in  Oakland cemetery; Grace Olin, born February 14th,  1890, in Elmdale, Coalcreek township, Ind. 
