1 NAME Henry Leonidas /Prentiss/
2 GIVN Henry Leonidas
2 SURN Prentiss
2 _MARNM Prentiss
2 NICK Prentis
1 NAME Major Henry L. Prentiss
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 4 JUL 1788
2 ADDR
3 CITY New London
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 NOV 1849
2 ADDR
3 CITY Quincy
3 STAE Illinois
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT '''Henry Leonidas Prentiss''' (1788–1849)—frequently styled in contemporary press reports and local history accounts as Major Henry L. Prentiss—was an early 19th-century American pioneer, businessman, and civic figure, best known 
2 CONC as the father of Union Major General Benjamin Mayberry Prentiss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and Birth Date Resolution:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was born in New London, New London County, Connecticut, on July 4, 1788. He was a son of John Prentiss V and Esther (Richards) Prentiss.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Genealogical Record Note: Early compiled volumes—notably C.J.F. Binney’s 19th-century Prentice/Prentiss Genealogy—printed a conflicting timeline by recording Henry's birth in July 1788 alongside a transcription of his father’s gr
2 CONC avestone date as November 22, 1780. Modern genealogical analysis resolves this biological impossibility as a common slate-headstone transcription error, where a weathered "1788" was misread as "1780." Correcting the father’s death
2 CONC  to November 22, 1788, aligns with Henry’s birth on July 4, 1788.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and the Mayberry Lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT During the War of 1812, Henry served in the Connecticut militia, rising from sergeant to captain. Following his move to western Virginia (now West Virginia), Henry married Rebecca Mayberry on April 14, 1814, in Belleville, Wood Co
2 CONC unty, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebecca Mayberry (c. 1795–c. 1860s): Born circa 1795 in Virginia, Rebecca was the daughter of George Mayberry and Rebecca (Pennybacker) Mayberry. Her maternal family were prominent Shenandoah Valley ironmasters—her grandfather, Di
2 CONC rck Pennybacker, and father, George Mayberry, built and operated the Columbia Furnace and Redwell Furnace in Virginia before her branch of the family joined the early 19th-century migration west to Wood County along the Ohio River
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Migration and Prominent Family Legacy:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Rebecca lived in Wood County, Virginia, before joining the westward movement into Marion County, Missouri, in 1836, and eventually settling permanently in Quincy, Adams County, Illinois, where Henry engaged in cordage ma
2 CONC nufacturing, land development, and local politics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Rebecca raised a family that achieved significant local and national prominence:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lucy (Prentiss) Bowles: Born in New London, Connecticut; settled in Adams County, Illinois, where she married pioneer farmer Augustin E. Bowles (1815–1872) as his second wife and raised seven children in Quincy.
2 CONT # Mary Catherine (Prentiss) Goodno: Born in Virginia (Wood County area); migrated westward with her parents through Missouri to Illinois, where she married into the Goodno family and resided in Quincy.
2 CONT # Maj. Gen. Benjamin Mayberry Prentiss (1819–1901): Born November 23, 1819, in Belleville, Virginia; worked in rope manufacturing in Quincy before rising to national renown as a Union Army Major General in the Civil War, famous fo
2 CONC r defending the "Hornet's Nest" at Shiloh (1862) and winning the Battle of Helena (1863).
2 CONT # Aurelia Prentiss (1821–1838): Born in Virginia; died as a teenager at age 17 in Marion County, Missouri, shortly after the family's westward migration out of Virginia and prior to their relocation to Quincy, Illinois.
2 CONT # Henry Clay Prentiss: Born during the family's Virginia/Missouri period; named in honor of statesman Henry Clay and worked alongside his father and brother Benjamin in cordage and rope manufacturing in Quincy.
2 CONT # Patrick Adair Prentiss: Born in Virginia during the family's residence in Wood County; recorded in early family registers alongside his brothers Benjamin and Henry Clay.
2 CONT # Henrietta Prentiss: Died in early childhood prior to the family's mid-1830s move west to Missouri and Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Murder in Quincy:
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Henry L. Prentiss met a tragic and violent death on Christmas Eve, December 24, 1849, in Quincy, Illinois, at age 61. Following an argument inside a local saloon on John's Square, he was ambushed outside and struck on the he
2 CONC ad by a young man named Austin West. He died four hours later from a skull fracture.
2 CONT
2 CONT His murder generated nationwide press coverage—including reports in New York's Ulster Telegraph—though distant syndicated papers frequently mangled the suspect's name as "Wert".
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/4:1:LWHN-ZXL
