2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben notes: There is no proof that he may have been in town during this event, at least as of yet (census records from 1860 show that his family was still there at that time, according to http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm
2 CONC .cgi?op=GET&db=dubart&id=I816), but here's an interesting historic footnote on his birthplace of Osceola, Missouri when he was about 7 years old (along with older brother James - age 9, and younger brother John - age 2). From the 
2 CONC Wikipedia page on the Sacking of Osceola (the Civil War era event): http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacking_of_Osceola
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sacking of Osceola was a Union Jayhawker initiative on September 23, 1861, to push out pro-Southern elements at Osceola, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Sterling Price's secessionist Missouri State Guard victory over General Nathaniel Lyon's Union army at the Battle of Wilson's Creek, Price began initiatives to "clean" out opposition in Kansas and retake the state of Mis
2 CONC souri.
2 CONT
2 CONT James H. Lane organized 1,200 troops to resist the Price invasion into Kansas. Price defeated Lane in the Battle of Dry Wood Creek near Fort Scott, Kansas. Lane retreated and Price continued his offensive further into Missouri to 
2 CONC the Siege of Lexington.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Price moved North, Lane launched an attack behind him. After crossing the Missouri border at Trading Post, Kansas on Sept 10, Lane began an offensive moving East on Butler, Harrisonville, Osceola and Clinton, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT The climax of the campaign was in September 23, 1861 at Osceola where Lane's forces drove off a small Southern force and then looted and burned the town. According to reports, many of the Kansans got so drunk that when it came tim
2 CONC e to leave they were unable to march and had to ride in wagons and carriages. They carried off with them a tremendous load of plunder, including as Lane's personal share a piano and a quantity of silk dresses. Hundreds of slaves f
2 CONC ollowed Lane to Kansas and freedom. The troops moved Northwest and arrived at Kansas City, Missouri on September 29 and was to pursue Price as he retreated south through the state.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lane's raid stirred hatred that would contribute to Quantrill's infamous raid on Lawrence, Kansas leading in turn to the depopulation of four counties of western Missouri in General Order No. 11 (1863, affecting Jackson, Cass, Bat
2 CONC es, and Vernon counties, not neighboring St. Clair County).
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular media
2 CONT
2 CONT The legend of the Osceola massacre served as a partial basis for the 1976 movie The Outlaw Josey Wales starring and directed by Clint Eastwood, in which the lead character is a Southern Missouri homesteader who wants to avenge his
2 CONC  family who died at the hands of Redlegs from Kansas.[1][2]
2 CONT
