1 NAME Edward Forrest /Ellis/ M.D.
2 GIVN Edward Forrest
2 SURN Ellis
2 NSFX M.D.
2 _MARNM Ellis
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Evergreen Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fayetteville
3 STAE Arkansas
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 19 AUG 1863
2 ADDR
3 STAE Missouri
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 AUG 1957
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fayetteville
3 STAE Arkansas
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From the biography on Edward Forrest Ellis, M.D. (1863-1957) published by the Arkansas Country Doctor Museum in Lincoln, Arkansas:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.drmuseum.net/ellis/Document%20%282%29.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Edward Forrest Ellis, beloved physician and surgeon of Fayetteville, Arkansas, was born August 19, 1863, in St. Clair County, Missouri. He was the son of Edward Syvanus Ellis and Martha Jane Stearns Ellis. He moved with his pa
2 CONC rents to Northwest Arkansas in 1866.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1885 he graduated from Missouri Medical College in St. Louis, which later became Washington University Medical School. In 1885, he became a member of the Washington County (Arkansas) Medical Society and the Arkansas State Medic
2 CONC al Society. Doctor Ellis practiced in Hindsville, Arkansas, for ten years; Springdale, Arkansas, until 1904; and Fayetteville, Arkansas, until his death on Aug. 8, 1957.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1900, Dr. Ellis graduated from the Chicago Polyclinic and attended the Chicago Post-Graduate School of Medicine. In 1902, he graduated from the Philadelphia Polyclinic and College for Graduates in Medicine. In 1904, he complete
2 CONC d a course in New York Post Graduate School of Medicine. He also attended, at various times, lecture courses and clinics at the Mayo Brothers Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota, St. Louis, Kansas City, New York, Philadelphia, and Chic
2 CONC ago. He held licenses to practice medicine in Arkansas, Missouri, Oklahoma, Mississippi, Texas, and Tennessee. He was the first physician to do major surgery in Northwest Arkansas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913, Dr. Ellis was appointed to the Arkansas State Board of Health by Gov. Futrell and reappointed in 1917 by Gov. Brough. He served on the Board for eight years and was "largely responsible for keeping standards of the profes
2 CONC sion high." During World War I, he served as Contract Surgeon and cared for soldiers at the ROTC camp at the University of Arkansas during a terrible flu epidemic. He also performed examinations for the County Draft Board during W
2 CONC orld War I and World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis held various offices, including the presidency, of the the Washington County Medical Society, and he was 1918-1919 President of the Arkansas Medical Society. He also served as President of the Tenth Councilor District Me
2 CONC dical Society. He was a delegate for the Arkansas Medical Society to American Medical Association conventions in Detroit and Philadelphia, and through the years attended many medical meetings in St. Louis, New Orleans, Chicago, Na
2 CONC shville, Kansas City, Oklahoma City, and Memphis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was signally honored by the State Medical Society. In 1921 when he was Treasurer of the State Medical Board, he was presented a handsome bronze loving cup. In 1932 at the conclusion of a Memorial Service he was presented a beau
2 CONC tiful bouquet of roses as a "token of appreciation" of his "long and faithful services to Organized Medicine." In 1948 the Society presented him with a very handsome watch engraved with the worlds, "with esteem and appreciation."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1916, Dr. Ellis was made a Fellow in the American College of Surgeons, and in 1941 he was a Founder Fellow in the International College of Surgeons. He was also a member of the Southern Medical Association, a member of the Amer
2 CONC ican Association of Railway Surgeons, and, at one time, President of the Frisco Medical Association. He served as Division Surgeon at Fayetteville of the St. Louis-San Francisco (Frisco) Railway from 1918 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1934, Dr. Ellis had completed 50 years in the practice of medicine and surgery, and he was awarded the Honorary Degree of Doctor of Laws by the University of Arkansas at Commencement exercises.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940, during the University presidency of J. William Fulbright, Dr. Ellis presented his medical library of more than 1,100 books to the University's Medical School in Little Rock. He was informed that the gift raised the Medica
2 CONC l Schools' standing. For a number of years, the collection was housed in a special "Ellis Room" in the School's library. Among the gifts given to the library was a complete set of the Year Books of Surgery. This was one of perhaps
2 CONC  only two complete sets existing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis' picture appeared in the Year Book of General Medicine for 1940, and in the preface to the volume it was stated that "...there is only one, as far as the records show, who, starting in 1901, has taken all the Year Books 
2 CONC through all the 40 years. Edward F. Ellis, former president of the Arkansas State Medical Society, is the single individual who, during a long lifetime of practice, has kept in touch with medical progress by means of the Year Book
2 CONC s from the time they first published." The Year Book publisher presented him with a gift of all succeeding copies to be published during his lifetime. Dr. Ellis also made a gift of some early surgical instruments to the Medical Sc
2 CONC hool.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis' son-in-law, Dr. Vincent O. Lesh, himself a graduate of Jefferson Medical College in Philadelphia, has said that Dr. Ellis was the best-read physician he had ever known.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1964 the Doctor Edward Forrest Ellis Memorial Fund was established at the University of Arkansas Medical Center with memorial gifts to the family after Dr. Ellis' death. The annual income from the fund is used to award surgical
2 CONC  textbooks to the third-year medical student who "shows the most promise in surgery."
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis was keenly interested in community affairs. He served on the Fayetteville School Board for 15 years, four of them as chairman. He was instrumental in acquiring the land and constructing the Harmon Playfield. He and Mrs. 
2 CONC Ellis were honored as two founders of the Playfield in 1956. He also aided in the fund-raising effort which brought the Western Methodist Assembly to Mt. Sequoyah in Fayetteville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis helped organize the First National Bank of Fayetteville in 1904 and served on its Board of Directors from the bank's founding until his death. He served as one of the bank's first presidents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis was a member of the Christian Church and was a 32nd Degree Mason and a Shriner. In 1945 he was made a Life Member of Washington Lodge No. 1 of the Masonic Order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis was on the staff of the Fayetteville City Hospital from its founding on July 1, 1912, and served as Chief of Staff for a number of years. His name headed the list of physicians at the hospital until his death in 1957. Th
2 CONC rough the years, he donated money and equipment to the hospital. He was also a member of the staff of the Washington County Hospital (now Washington Regional Hospital) since its inception.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1953, he was president of the Fifty Year (Physician's) Club in Arkansas. On his 90th birthday, Aug. 19, 1953, he was honored by the Washington County Historical Society, receiving a "Distinguished Citizen" citation at a ceremon
2 CONC y on the lawn of his home attended by more than 400.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1956, on the 75th anniversary of his graduation, he was made a lifetime member of the Washington University Medical School Alumni Association.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps Dr. Ellis' most lasting achievement is the influence he had on other physicans. Through the years, he was often sought in consultation. Through the years, he was often sought in consultation. Medical students liked to acco
2 CONC mpany him "on his rounds" and observe him in surgery and a number of them later achieved distinction in their chosen fields. One letter to him - this from a local physician - sums up his influence: "Every time, I approached the ho
2 CONC spital it is, and has been, and will be, to learn something from you and it. If you will but understand that you are the only doctor friend I have that has seen fit to teach me, and the only one that I depend on for consolation an
2 CONC d help as well as advice, you will understand why I write this letter."
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis was married twice, first in 1888 to Miss Mable Stafford of Springdale and, after her death, to Miss Cener Holcombe of Fayetteville in 1900. His children are 
2 CONT *1. Forrest Ellis Gardner, 
2 CONT *2. Earl Ellis, 
2 CONT *3. Martha Belle Ellis Hays, 
2 CONT *4. Elizabeth Ellis, and 
2 CONT *5. Dr. Ruth Ellis Lesh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandchildren are Dr. Ellis Gardner, Charles Earl Gardner, Dr. Ruth Elizabeth Ellis, Mary Carolyn Ellis, Vincent Edward Lesh, Susan Elizabeth Lesh Chadick, Donald Edward Hays, and William Fleming Hays. There are several great-gran
2 CONC dchildren. Dr. Ellis was very proud that his wife and all of his children attended the University of Arkansas, and that his daughter, Ruth, his grandson, Ellis Gardner, and his grand-daughter, Ruth Elizabeth Ellis, all became doct
2 CONC ors of medicine.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the County Historical Society's ceremony awarding Dr. Ellis the "Distinguished Citizen" citation, Prof. F. S. Root, long-time Superintendent of the Fayetteville Public Schools, now retired, said that Dr. Ellis was "the embodime
2 CONC nt of scholarship, integrity, sympathy, devotion, and Christian faith."
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From Centennial History of Arkansas:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/centennialhistor00hern#page/1134
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARD F. ELLIS, M. D. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 1904 Dr. Edward F. Ellis has been practicing medicine in Fayetteville and the consensus of public opinion places him in the foremost rank of professional men in the state. A native of Missouri, he was born in St. Clair count
2 CONC y, August 19, 1863, a son of Edward S. and Martha (Stearns) Ellis, the former a native of Tennessee and the latter of Missouri. Their marriage was celebrated in Missouri and there the father engaged in farming until 1861, when he 
2 CONC joined the Confederate army. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the close of the war he came to Arkansas and bought land in Washington county, where both he and his wife continued to reside until called to their final rest. Five children were born to their union, three of whom are living
2 CONC : Edward F., whose name initiates this review; William E., who is in the government service in Colorado; and Mark St. Clair, who is living retired in Fayetteville. The last named was for thirty years in the United States navy. Thr
2 CONC oughout his life Edward S. Ellis was a stanch supporter of the democratic party and a consistent member of the Christian church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the acquirement of an education Edward F. Ellis attended the common schools of Springdale and later, after determining to become a physician, he entered the Missouri Medical College at St. Louis. He was graduated in 1885 and im
2 CONC mediately began practice in Hindsville, Arkansas, where he remained for a decade. The year 1896 witnessed his removal to Springdale, where he continued to follow his profession until 1904, when he came to Fayetteville and has prac
2 CONC ticed here since. He makes a specialty of surgery and his courteous sympathy and his professional skill have gained him distinctive precedence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1888 occurred the marriage of Dr. Ellis and Miss Mabel Stafford and they became the parents of two children: Forrest, the wife of Guy Gardner, a prominent hardware man at Russellville; and Earl, engaged in the automobile busine
2 CONC ss in Fayetteville. The wife and mother departed this life in 1896. Eight years later Dr. Ellis was again married, his second union being with Miss Cener Holcomb, a native of Springdale, Arkansas. Three children have been born to 
2 CONC them: Martha Belle, Elizabeth and Ruth, all at home. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis votes with the democratic party but has never taken an active part in politics, aside from exercising his right of franchise in support of measures which he deems of value in good government. He is now president of the F
2 CONC ayetteville school board and is a member of the board of health and pension examiners. Fraternally he is a Knights-Templar Mason and a member of the Mystic Shrine. In the line of his profession he holds membership in the Washingto
2 CONC n County Medical Society, the Arkansas State Medical Society, of which he was at one time president, the American Medical Association and the Southern Medical Association. He is also a fellow of the American College of Surgery. He
2 CONC  is likewise prominent in financial circles as a director of the First National Bank. In professional circles his worth and ability are widely recognized and that public opinion is favorable to him is indicated in the liberal patr
2 CONC onage accorded him. He keeps in touch with what is being done by eminent members of the profession throughout the country and his perusal of the best medical literature has brought him wide knowledge of scientific principles. In h
2 CONC is practice he is always careful in the diagnosis of his cases and conscientious in the performance of his professional duties. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Harrison Times of Harrison, Arkansas, on Thursday, Aug. 8, 1957:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pioneer Doctor Edward Ellis dies at Fayetteville
2 CONT
2 CONT Fayetteville, Ark. (AP) - Dr. Edward Forest Ellis, 94, one of Arkansas' oldest practicing physicians, died suddnely at his home here yesterday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Ellis had practiced medicine in Northwest Arkansas since his graduation from the Missouri Medical College in 1865. He came to Washington County in 1896 and moved here in 1904.
2 CONT
2 CONT He helped found the Fayetteville City Hospital in 1912 and was active on its staff until his death. He last held regular office hours Monday.
2 CONT
2 CONT A former president of the Arkansas Medical Society and the Washington County Medical Society, Dr. Ellis also held many high national offices. He had been a fellow in the American College of Surgeons since 1916 and in 1941 he becam
2 CONC e a founding fellow in the International College of Surgeons.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was awarded an honorary L.L.D. degree from the University of Arkansas in 1934 and later he donated his fine medical library to the university's School of Medicine.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born Aug. 19, 1863, in St. Clair County, Missouri, but his parents moved to Northwest Arkansas, in 1866.
