1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Mark St. Clair Ellis Timeline'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Childhood'''
2 CONT *1873, March 30: Born in Fayetteville, Washington County, Arkansas to farmer Edward Silvanus Ellis and Martha Jane Stearns as their fourth child (they were married 11 years already, by 1870, they had already removed from Osceola i
2 CONC n St. Clair County, Missouri, to Arkansas to avoid anti-Southern sentiment in that state). The child is brought home to the family farm northeast of White River in Washington County, Arkansas.
2 CONT *1877, Feb. 15: Martha Jane, Mark's mother, dies, leaving Edward to look after the three boys (Edward, Willie, and Mark). 
2 CONT *1880, June 12: Mark (age 3) was living in Bush Creek Township, Washington County, Arkansas, with family members: Father Edward Ellis, brothers Edward F. and Willie. Lived adjacent to Stinson S. Stearns, Mark's grandfather, and Ma
2 CONC nerva Jane Reed (Stearns), Mark's grandmother. Moses Stearns, Mark's uncle, still lived with Stinson and Manerva. (There were also Reed families adjacent to them.) 
2 CONT *1882, September: Edward (age 45), father of Mark (age 9), marries Fannie Mullins (age 26), daughter of Judge Thomas Mullins. She had given birth to Thomas Clifford on June 18, before the couple could marry. Later, the couple give
2 CONC  birth to the family's first daughter, but Jessie, the girl, dies at age 3. Mark's father takes part in both the International Order of Odd Fellows and in the local Christian Church; community members later describe him as "honora
2 CONC ble".
2 CONT *1888: Older brother of Mark (age 15), Edward (age 19), marries Mabel Amanda Stafford (age 20). The following June, down in Hindsville, they have their first child, a girl they named Johnnie.
2 CONT *1888, Nov. 11: Mark's older brother, Willie (age 20), marries Virginia Elizabeth Bland (age 20). They have their first child the following August, a boy they named Floyd.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early Years in US Navy'''
2 CONT *1892, July 1: Mark Saint Clair Ellis (age 19) admitted to the US Naval Academy in Annapolis, Maryland. This marks his date of entry into the service.
2 CONT *1896, June: Mark (age 23) graduates from the US Naval Academy in Annapolis, Maryland. He is one of 25 of his class assigned to Line Divisions afloat. He was 19th among those going to the Line, with scores: First year: 59.36 (out 
2 CONC of 76), Second year: 114.35 (out of 152), Third year: 169.79 (out of 228), Fourth year: 223.13 (out of 304), and four-year aggregate: 566.63 (out of 760).
2 CONT *1897, Jan. 1: Mark has been stationed on board the cruiser USS Brooklyn, under Capt. Francis A. Cook. He served alongside cadets Andrew E. Kalbach, James H. Roys, and Roland I Curtin. The ship was unassigned at time of publicatio
2 CONC n. (It eventually serves as part of the representation of the United States at British Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee on June 22.)
2 CONT *1898, Jan. 1: Mark (age 24) is listed as a Naval Cadet who has passed the requisite academic course and is now performing two years service at sea (or pursuing special studies prior to final graduation). He has 2 years and 3 mont
2 CONC hs sea service, 3 years and 2 months shore or other duty, and was unemployed 1 month. He didn't have a present duty assignment.
2 CONT *1898, Apr. 9: Mark (age 25) is assigned to the USS Hornet, a converted yacht (formerly named Alicia, purchased by the US Navy from Henry Morrison Flagler three days before his assignment to the boat). He was probably present for 
2 CONC the commissioning of the boat three days later in New York City, and he sailed under James Meredith Helm, its first commander, alongside Christopher C. Fewel and Ralph Earle. This appears to be his first combat sea service assignm
2 CONC ent.
2 CONT *1898, April 18: The Hornet joins the fleet blockading Cuba in the Spanish-American War.
2 CONT *1898, April 24: The Hornet beaches for the first time at Havana. It carries out several short trips between Key West and Cuba.
2 CONT *1898, May 6: Mark St. Clair Ellis becomes an Ensign, advancing from the rank of Cadet in his first promotion. 
2 CONT *1898, June 28: The 500-ton Spanish steamer Benito Estenger and small sloop Emanuel y Raoul are captured by the USS Hornet off Manzanillo, Cuba.
2 CONT *1898, July 1: Ensign Mark St. Clair Ellis is listed as still aboard the USS Hornet. However, he had left the converted yacht before the previous day's attack on the Spanish port of Manzanillo, Cuba (part of its patrol and reconna
2 CONC issance duties enforcing a blockade on the southern coast of Cuba). The boat had been damaged in the attack, and had to be towed out of the battle by the ship Wompatuck (one sailor was scalded to death when the main steam pipe was
2 CONC  struck, with two others badly injured).
2 CONT *1898, July 2: Mark and a prize crew sails the steamer Benito Estenger and small sloop Emanuel y Raoul into Key West.
2 CONT *1898, July 3: The Battle of Santiago de Cuba took place, involving the pre-dreadnought battleship USS Oregon chasing down the cruiser Cristobal Colon. Sometime after this, somehow, Mark deploys to the Far East; he was photographe
2 CONC d on board the pre-dreadnough battleship USS Oregon with Capt. Charles Edgar Clark (trainer), Cadet Luther Martin Overstreet, and a midshipman named Haight. (The journal of Cadet Cyrus Robinson Clark suggests this picture was take
2 CONC n just after the Battle of Santiago in July 1898. Miller and Overstreet are listed as being on board the Oregon in the Pacific Station as of Jan. 1, 1898, while the ship was stationed in the Pacific prior to its 66-day record-sett
2 CONC ing journey from San Francisco to Jupiter Inlet, Florida; Ellis was not yet deployed anywhere at that time.)
2 CONT *1898, Sept. 12: According to the ship's log book, Ensign Mark St. C. Ellis, USN, reported on board the training ship USS Vicksburg for duty. The ship was moored at Kittery, Maine.
2 CONT *1898, Oct. 16: The USS Vicksburg begins its training voyage at Norfolk, Virginia. Mark serves under Cdr. Abraham B. H. Lillie.
2 CONT *1899, Jan. 1: Mark continues to be on board the Training Ship USS Vicksburg under Cdr. Abraham B. H. Lillie as of the publication of the 1899 US House of Representatives Register of the Commissioned and Warrant Officers of the Un
2 CONC ited States Navy and Marine Corps. He is listed as having been 8 months in present sea service (which dates to his assignment to the Hornet), 3 years total sea service, and 3 years 6 months shore or other duty.
2 CONT *1899, Jan. 17: The USS Vicksburg was off Fredericksted, St. Croix. Ensign M. St. C. Ellis signed the log entry for that day.
2 CONT *1899, Feb. 4: The USS Vicksburg, along with the USS Annapolis, round Cape San Juan. Ensign Ellis conducts weekly inspections of compartments and mechanical devices, alongside Ensign Baldwin.
2 CONT *1899, Feb. 13: At San Juan, Puerto Rico, the USS Vicksburg takes on from the USS Panther 300 lbs of rice. Ensign Ellis signs the log book.
2 CONT *1899, Feb. 25: Off La Guayra, Venezuela, the USS Vicksburg encounters the Royal mail steamer Avon. Ensigns Ellis and Baldwin conduct weekly inspections of compartments and mechanical devices, finding them in good condition.
2 CONT *1899, Mar. 12-20: At Kingston, Jamaica, Ensigns Ellis and Baldwin conduct regular weekly inspections.
2 CONT *1899, Mar. 25: At Santiago de Cuba, Ensigns Ellis and Baldwin conduct their weekly inspection as the ship meets the USS Yankton.
2 CONT *1899, Apr. 2: The USS Vicksburg completes its training voyage upon arrival in Havana, Cuba. Ensign Mark St. C. Ellis remains on board the ship for the return voyage.
2 CONT *1899, Apr. 8: The USS Vicksburg conducts small arms target practice off St. Petersburg, Florida, with Ensign Ellis in one of the firing parties.
2 CONT *1899, Apr. 15-22: The USS Vicksburg anchors off Key West. Ensigns Baldwin and Ellis conduct their weekly inspections.
2 CONT *1899, Apr. 29: The USS Vicksburg is heading for New York, and has reached Bostwick's Bay. It continues past Oyster Bay and Hampstead Harbor on the way in. Ensigns Ellis and Baldwin conduct their weekly inspections.
2 CONT *1899, May 24: The USS Vicksburg discharges Ensign M. St. C. Ellis in order to transfer him to the auxiliary cruiser USS Buffalo, alongside Lt. J. B. Blish. The ship was still en route to New York on a return voyage from Manila (i
2 CONC t had left the previous December, and the ship immediately returned as of May 7).
2 CONT *1899, June 19: Following an eight-week kidney-related illness that had him holed up at the home of Mark's elder brother, Edward, in Springdale, Arkansas, Marks' father, Edward Silvanus Ellis, died at age 62. Despite the brother b
2 CONC eing a medical doctor, nothing could be done to save him. He was buried at Gearin's Chapel near Fayetteville.
2 CONT *1899, July 3: Mark (age 26), after the USS Buffalo was placed out of commission for the forseeable future following its return to the United States from Manila, is transferred to the hospital ship USS Solace, under the command of
2 CONC  Cdr. Andrew Dunlap. The ship was photographed off Mare Island a couple days earlier before putting to sea. It may have been on the Solace that he was taken to the Far East.
2 CONT *1900, Jan. 1: Mark is serving in the Asiatic Station (most likely the Philippines) on board the gunboat USS Bennington under Cdr. Conway H. Arnold. He currently has 1 year and 7 months sea service under his current commission, an
2 CONC d 4 years 2 months at sea for his full career.
2 CONT *1900, April 6: The armored monitor USS Monterey, which left the Continental United States on June 11, 1899, sets sail from the Phillipines for Hong Kong. Mark (age 27) may have been on board this vessel by this time.
2 CONT *1900, July 6: Mark was serving as an Ensign on board the USS Monterey (Captain George W. Pigman), stationed in Hong Kong, where the ship was receiving new boilers. Afterward, his ship served as the station ship for Shanghai.
2 CONT *1900, July 25: The USS Monterey sails from Shanghai upriver to Nanking with Special Commissioner T. S. Sharretts on board for a diplomatic mission.
2 CONT *1900, July 31: The USS Monterey returns to Shanghai. It conducts operations between Chefoo and Hong Kong, and occasionally serves as station ship in Shanghai.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''First marriage of Lt. Ellis with Elizabeth Tilghman'''
2 CONT *1902, Oct. 6: Mark (age 29) is stationed on board the gunboat USS Bancroft, which was recommissioned to serve as the station ship at San Juan, Puerto Rico. The ship frequently cruises the West Indies from this port.
2 CONT *1903, July 19: Mark (age 30) was on the Bancroft during its cruise up the Orinoco River to rescue American nationals from the fighting taking place there (amid torrential rains on the 17th and 18th), and he described the Battle o
2 CONC f Ciudad Bolivar (an under-discussed civil strife engagement in the Venezuelan Crisis of 1902-03): "No better view of the whole battle could possibly have been obtained than that from our ship," wrote Lieutenant Mark St.Clair Elli
2 CONC s, "which was so close to the shore that individuals could be recognized through the [long] glass. The ship was frequently struck by bullets, which added the spice of personal danger to the picturesque tragedy we beheld." 
2 CONT *1903, July 25: Mark wrote for the Port-of-Spain Herald about intelligence gathered that traitors to the rebel cause had sold out the defenses of the Zamuro fortress at Ciudad Bolivar.  The USS Bancroft, his ship, later cruised al
2 CONC ong the coasts of Columbia and the newly independent Panama during the latter's break from the former.
2 CONT *1903, Nov. 12: Mark returns to the United States and '''marries Elizabeth Tilghman''' (age 33, widowed) in Manhattan, probably during a furlough. He returns to the USS Bancroft.
2 CONT *1904, Jan. 1: Mark, still an Ensign, remains on board the USS Bancroft, under Lt. Cdr. Abraham E. Culver, still stationed at San Juan, Puerto Rico (on temporary assignment with the Carribean Squadron). Over the coming year, his s
2 CONC tepmother, Fannie Ellis, dies and is buried near Fayetteville.
2 CONT *1904, July 1: Recently promoted Lt. Mark St. Clair Ellis (Age 31) is assigned on board the USS Nevada (six-gun, 3,214-ton, 2,400 hp twin screw monitor), under the command of Cdr. Alfred Reynolds. The ship operates in the Coast Sq
2 CONC uadron of the US Navy.
2 CONT *1905, Jan. 1: Mark is still on board the USS Nevada (still part of the Coast Squadron) according to the 1905 US House of Representatives Register of the Commissioned and Warrant Officers of the United States Navy and Marine Corps
2 CONC . He had, at this time, 1 year and 10 months sea service in his present commission, 7 years and 10 months sea service in his total commission, 4 years and 2 months shore and other duty, and 0 years 6 months unemployment in his tot
2 CONC al career. He was No. 237 in grade.
2 CONT *1906, Feb. 7: Mark (age 33) returns from sea service on board the USS Nevada, the ship having been commanded by Cdr. Alfred Reynolds. His term of duty recorded as ending in March, but on this date, the Little Rock Battle-Ax descr
2 CONC ibed his visit home: "Lieut. Mark St. C. Ellis, U. S. Marine Corps (sic), and formerly of this State, is visiting friends in town. Lieut. Ellis is of distinguished and graceful military bearing, and is a great favorite with the fa
2 CONC ir sex, who are all captivated by his manly charms. While recently passing through St. Louis he had a large hop given him at the Southern Hotel."
2 CONT *1906, April: Mark goes on inspection duty within the Navy's Bureau of Equipment in Philadelphia, alongside Lt. Cdr. William G. Miller, retired Chief Boatswain Peter H. Smith, and retired Chief Sailmaker Charles H. Jones.
2 CONT *1907, Jan. 1: Mark remains on inspection duty within the Navy's Bureau of Equipment still in Philadelphia, alongside retired Chief Sailmaker Charles H. Jones (who also is on duty in Wilmington, Delaware, and Camden, New Jersey). 
2 CONC He has on his record 3 years of sea duty under the current commission, 9 years total sea service, 5 years and 11 months of shore or other duty, and 6 months unemployed.
2 CONT *1907, July 1: Mark (age 34) remains on inspection duty in the Navy's Bureau of Equipment, again, still in Philadelphia alongside retired Chief Sailmaker Charles H. Jones.
2 CONT *1907, Nov. 18: Mark finishes his inspection duties in Philadelphia and is stationed with the Second Division on board the pre-dreadnought first-class battleship USS Virginia, just before its departure as part of the Great White F
2 CONC leet.
2 CONT *1907, Dec. 16-23: The Great White Fleet departs Hampton Roads, Virginia. Over the next week, it would sail more than 1800 nautical miles (roughly 3,300 kilometers). The Virginia sails under Seaton Schroeder.
2 CONT *1907, Dec. 29 - 1908, Jan. 12: After a week in Port of Spain, the Great White Fleet sails from Trinidad southward some 3,400 nautical miles (roughly 6,300 kilometers).
2 CONT *1908, Jan. 1: Mark was listed as a Lieutenant within the 1908 US House of Representatives Register of the Commissioned and Warrant Officers of the United States Navy and Marine Corps.
2 CONT *1908, Jan. 21 - Feb. 1: After nine days in Rio de Janeiro, The Great White Fleet sails southward past Argentina on its way to the Pacific Ocean, covering some 2,400 nautical miles (4,400 kilometers) to get to the next port of cal
2 CONC l.
2 CONT *1908, Feb. 7-20: After a week in Punta Arenas, the Great White Fleet sails northward along the Chilean coast, covering some 2,900 nautical miles (5,300 kilometers) in just under two weeks.
2 CONT *1908, Feb. 29 - Mar. 12: After nine days in Callao, the closest port to Lima, Peru, the great white fleet begins a journey of 3,000 nautical miles (5,600 kilometers) out of South America.
2 CONT *1908, Apr. 11 - May 6: After a month in the Mexican port of Magdalena Bay, the Great White fleet sails toward America, covering some 1,100 nautical miles (2,000 kilometers) in just under a month.
2 CONT *1908, May 6: The Great White Fleet completes the first leg of its circumnavigation in San Francisco. Over the next couple days, a Naval Review is held in San Francisco Bay for Secretary of the Navy Victor H. Metcalf, one that inc
2 CONC ludes the armored cruiser USS California. After this time, likely, Mark (age 35) was transferred from the battleship USS Virginia to that ship, which did not take part in the Great White Fleet.
2 CONT *1908, May 23: After more than two weeks in port, the Great White Fleet sails northward to Puget Sound. The USS Virginia remains in San Francisco and the new battleship USS Nevada takes its place instead for the Second Leg of its 
2 CONC circumnavigation. 
2 CONT *1908, July 7-16: After returning to San Francisco from Seattle, the Great White Fleet sails westward from San Francisco on the Third Leg of its circumnavigation. Mark was on board the USS California by this time; he remained on t
2 CONC he west coast as the fleet sailed on. (This transfer was apparently unplanned, as Mark's name remains on the list of officers for the USS Virginia under Capt. Seaton Schroeder in a "Souvenir of the Visit of America's Fleet" that w
2 CONC as printed for the fleet's Australia visit, but considering the chronology, he quite apparently didn't continue on past San Francisco.)
2 CONT *1909, Jan. 1: Mark is 6th in Grade as a Lieutenant (4 years and 2 months in sea service under present commission, 10 years and 2 months of total sea service, 6 years and 8 months of shore or other duty, and 7 months unemployed - 
2 CONC promotion is pending) and remains on board the armored cruiser USS California, part of the Second Divison of the Pacific Fleet.
2 CONT *1909, Fall: While Mark (age 36) is on board, the USS California takes part on a cruise to Hawaii and Samoa before returning to the west coast for further training and drills.
2 CONT *1910, May 4: Mark (age 37) is a naval officer stationed on board the Pennsylvania-class armored cruiser USS California at Mare Island in Vallejo Township, Solano County, California. 
2 CONT *1910, June 1: Mark, a Lieutenant Commander by this time, files a patent for a Self-Registering target, and assigns it to the Self-Scoring Target Company, a corporation of California. Patented 0011.3,1911.
2 CONT *1910, Aug. 26: Mark is transferred from the USS California, and is placed in charge of the naval magazine at Mare Island in California.
2 CONT *1911, Jan. 1: Mark remains in charge of the naval magazine at Mare Island (serving with Chief Gunner Augustus Anderson). He is 145th in grade (promotion not likely pending), he has 1 year 2 months of sea service in this commissio
2 CONC n (since May 27, 1909), 12 years and 3 months total sea service, 6 years and 1 month of shore and other duty, and 7 months unemployed.
2 CONT *1912, Jan. 1: Mark is still in charge of the naval magazine at Mare Island. He is 93rd in grade (promotion still unlikely), he has 1 year 2 months of sea service in this commission (since May 27, 1909), 12 years and 3 months tota
2 CONC l sea service, 7 years and 1 month of shore and other duty, and 7 months unemployed.
2 CONT *1912, April 15: HMS Titanic sinks.
2 CONT *1912, April 27: Mark (age 39) submits a patent for a "Sanitary Finger-Moistener" ("hand or desk devices of the office or personal type for applying liquid, other than ink, by contact to surfaces, e.g., for applying adhesive"). It
2 CONC 's granted on Aug. 13.
2 CONT *1912, Nov. 23: Mark was assigned to the cruiser USS Maryland, which was assigned to surveying duties in Alaska.
2 CONT *1913, Jan. 1: Mark St. Clair Ellis is listed as a Lieutenant Commander in the 1913 US House of Representatives Register of the Commissioned and Warrant Officers of the United States Navy and Marine Corps. His ship, the Maryland, 
2 CONC patrolled off the Mexican and Central American coast during times of crises to help rescue Americans stranded in the Civil Wars affecting these countries.
2 CONT *1914, Jan. 1: Lt. Cdr. Mark St. Clair Ellis (age 40) still serves on board the cruiser USS Maryland.
2 CONT *1915, Nov. 26: Mark (age 42) is promoted to Commander. He becomes captain of the Pacific Fleet-based transport USS Buffalo, succeeding Montgomery M. Taylor. The ship serves as an auxiliary cruiser.
2 CONT *1915, Nov. 29: The USS Buffalo rejoins the US Pacific Fleet after ten months refitting, and it sails to waters off Mexico.
2 CONT *1916, Jan. 1: Cdr. Mark St. Clair Ellis (the second lowest grade commander, due to his complete lack of seniority - he was promoted only a month earlier) continues to command the transport USS Buffalo.
2 CONT *1916, July 17: Cdr. Mark St. Clair Ellis (age 43) steps down as commander of the transport USS Buffalo. He is succeeded by Pope Washington. 
2 CONT *1916, Sept. 20: Elizabeth (age 46) hosts with her husband Mark a dinner to honor the return of Cdr. Adalbert Althouse, who commanded the USS Cleveland during its recent cruise off the coast of Mexico.
2 CONT *1918, Jan. 1: Following the end of his command on the USS Buffalo, Mark (age 44) has remained unassigned. Still, he is 21st in grade as of this year.
2 CONT *1918, Feb 5: Mark's wife Elizabeth dies at age 48 and is later buried at Arlington National Cemetery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Second marriage with Lena Alma Allen, widow of Titanic victim Hugh Roscoe Rood'''
2 CONT *1918, May 20: Mark (age 45) marries '''Lena Alma Allen Rood''' (age 56, an HMS Titanic widow, of Hugh Roscoe Rood, 1st class passenger and Seattle creosote tycoon) in Bern, Switzerland. Soon after, he was appointed the second com
2 CONC mandant of US Naval Training Camp Begley in Charleston (Lena was photographed giving awards to recruits for athletic achievements shortly after their marriage). In his address to recruits on assuming command, he said: "Somebody ha
2 CONC s to do the work in the lower ratings, so why not you. Try to make it said of the bluejacket in this day what was current in the palmy days of ancient Rome, 'When to be a Roman soldier was greater than to be a King.'"
2 CONT *1918, Sept. 27: Mark succeeds Farmer Morrison as Captain of the sloop-of-war steamer USS Hartford (formerly the flagship of David Farragut during the Battle of Mobile Bay in 1864). He commanded until an unknown date before the sh
2 CONC ip was decommissioned (Aug. 20, 1926). The ship had been used as a station ship for Charleston, South Carolina, for six years already; likely, the command of the ship came with command of the Charleston Shipyard, as Mark also serv
2 CONC ed as commander there at the same time (the ship was his headquarters). Shortly after Mark's arrival, the ship was visited in the shipyard by a young artist named Norman Rockwell, who had been deployed to Ireland from New York Cit
2 CONC y to paint insignia on aircraft (he still painted for the Saturday Evning Post). His ship took refuge in Charleston while an American submarine tried to intercept a German sub off the coast. He remained with Cdr. Ellis, who had hi
2 CONC s studio moved to the Hartford during his stay.
2 CONT *1918, Nov. 12: World War I ends, and Norman Rockwell is discharged at Charleston. Rockwell described the USS Hartford: "In the center of the ship a grand, red carpeted staircase swept down to a huge ballroom whose walls were deco
2 CONC rated with ornate, hand carved scrollwork. The staterooms, which were the officer’s quarters, were lavishly appointed and hung with all manner of rich velvets and tapestries. Down all the carpeted hallways ran handrails of gleamin
2 CONC g brass. The kitchen was staffed by a horde of cooks, meat cooks, pastry cooks, vegetable cooks, salad cooks, sauce cooks, etc. A marine band, scarlet jackets, blue trousers, white belts, and all, was kept always at the readiness 
2 CONC and marines in full dress uniforms, dark blue coats, light blue trousers guarded the gangplanks."
2 CONT *1919, Jan. 1: Commander Mark St. Clair Ellis is 12th in grade (promotion seemed imminent).
2 CONT *1919, October: Mark (age 46) takes leave of his duty station at Charleston, South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Third marriage with divorcee Mary Lawrence Taylor, retirement from US Navy'''
2 CONT *1920, Jan. 1: Commander Mark St. Clair Ellis is 9th in grade (promotion still imminent, post war). He has 1 year of sea service in grade, 15 years and 8 months total sea service, 10 years and 10 months of shore and other duty, an
2 CONC d 11 months unemployed. At the time of the publication, he was on sick leave.
2 CONT *1920, Feb. 27: Mark (age 46) applied for a passport in New York City, with the intention of traveling to Britain and France to consult nerve specialists, then onward to Italy and Switzerland for travel and recreation. Given the e
2 CONC vents that followed in his marriage, he probably traveled alone without his wife Lena (age 57). (According to a note found after her death, she may have been seeing Peter I, the widowed last King of Serbia, who she apparently inte
2 CONC racted with between December 1918 and July 1919, when he traveled abroad; he might have suggested marriage to her by letter, either in geste or as a serious proposal, after her apparent breakup with Mark, but of course this would 
2 CONC have been before the king's death in Belgrade on Aug. 16, 1921.)
2 CONT *1920, Aug. 6: Commander Mark St. Clair Ellis (age 47) retired from the United States Navy. He was probably in Europe at the time.
2 CONT *1922, Sept. 20: Mark (age 49) apparently married 2-time divorcee '''Mary Lawrence Taylor Wilcox''' (age 50). However, it appears that he wasn't yet divorced from Lena (age 60). (Lena would later say that she finally divorced Mark
2 CONC  following lawsuits brought on by another woman.)
2 CONT *1923: Mark (age 50) divorces from Mary Lawrence Taylor (age 51) shortly before she died at sea from a stroke that September. 
2 CONT *1923, Dec. 9: Mark and Lena sell their home on Spring Street in Fayetteville, Arkansas, to Mr. and Mrs. G. A. London. The Northwest Arkansas Times suggests that the Ellises have moved into a new home in Stresa, Italy, at this tim
2 CONC e. Mark disappears from the public record over the coming nine years.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fourth and final marriage, to Rose Katz St. John-Mildmay, later years'''
2 CONT *1932, Mar. 15: '''Lena Allen Stoiber Rood Ellis''' (age 70) finally grants a divorce to Mark (age 59) through a Swiss court. Lena goes into seclusion at her villa on the shores of Lake Maggiore in the northern Italian resort town
2 CONC  of Stresa, associating only with a hotel porter that she made into her secretary. She petitions to change her last name back to Stoiber, rather than retain the name Ellis. According to courtroom assertions by Mark, Lena promised 
2 CONC to pay a promissory note of $100,000 upon her death, if she didn't have it paid off before. (The sum was never paid.)
2 CONT *1933, Oct. 26: Mark (age 60) is issued Passport 5535 at Paris, France.
2 CONT *1934, Jan. 22: Mark arrived at New York on board the SS Manhattan, sailing from Havre, France. He is listed alone on the US citizen manifest, but may have been traveling with future wife Rose St. Jonn-Mildmay (age 54) to the Unit
2 CONC ed States in order to get married (Rose was living in Marnes-la-Coquette near Paris at the time).
2 CONT *1934, Mar. 26: Mark (age 61, residing in New York City) marries '''Rose J. Katz St. John-Mildmay''' in Benton County, Arkansas. She had been in country from the start of the year. (She had attempted to visit the United States in 
2 CONC 1922, still married to Henry E. St. John-Mildmay, who married her in the summer of 1914 in the St. Martin District of London - they likely divorced before Winter 1924.)
2 CONT *1934, Oct. 3: Newlywed wife Rose (age 55) left the United States, likely with Mark accompanying, and probably for France.
2 CONT *1934, Nov. 21: Rose receives her immigrant visa from the US Embassy in Paris (NQIV #187)
2 CONT *1935, Feb. 25 to March 10: Mark (age 61) sailed from Havre, France on board the SS City of Havre with wife Rose J. Katz St. John-Mildmay (claimed birth in Frankfort-on-Main, Germany, of English parents - however, her answer on th
2 CONC e 1940 census was inconsistent with this assertion). Also traveled with motion picture director Benjamin Darewsky, stateless person born in Rostov-na-Donu in 1903 traveling to Los Angeles to join his father.
2 CONT *1935, Mar. 27: Lena Allen Stoiber Rood Ellis died at her villa in the northern Italian resort town of Stresa, at age 72. She had been living in seclusion following her divorce with Mark (age 62), which was only recently granted. 
2 CONT *1935, April 1: Mark lived with Rose in New York City, according to 1940 Census.
2 CONT *1935, July 8: Mark entered into a contract with W. C. Jackson to have Jackson's company build tourist camps for the commander. These were completed while he was away from the county (after July 19), but payment for materials was 
2 CONC not forthcoming, according to Jackson. Ellis claimed he paid for the work as a lump sum, and that the materials were to be included in that lump sum.
2 CONT *1935, Aug. 6: Mark disputes the last will of Lena Stoiber in Denver County court on the basis that she owed him on a $136,000 promissory note, a claim the courts later rejected.
2 CONT *1935, Nov. 15: W. C. Jackson files a claim with the clerk and recorder of Washington County.
2 CONT *1936, Aug. 29: W. C. Jackson south to enforce a materialman's lien on Mark (age 63). In less than a month, on Sept. 23, Ellis files a demurrer to the complaint. This was overruled.
2 CONT *1938, Jan. 17: Mark filed suit against the Fayetteville Lumber Cement Company (and owner W. C. Jackson), which was appealed to the Supreme Court of Arkansas.
2 CONT *1940, April 6: Mark (age 67) lived in Prairie Township, Washington County, Arkansas, with wife Rose (age 60, claims birth in Italy, supposedly spoke Italian as a kid, married twice before with first marriage at age 17 or in 1901,
2 CONC  which would be the year 1899, no children, claims her parents were from New York).
2 CONT *1945, March 23: Mark (age 72) and Rose return from "the Ozark Mountains in Arkansas" to Wardour after spending their winter in "better climes".
2 CONT *1945, Oct. 12: Mark hosts a luncheon for his US Naval Academy classmates from the Class of 1896 who are still alive. His guests include Admiral Wat Tyle Cluverius (Jr.), president of the Worcester Polytechnical Institute (11th in
2 CONC  class); Capt. Dudley W. Knox, in charge of the office of History and records in the US Naval Department (17th in class), and his wife; the widow of Capt. Arthur MacArthur, Jr. (the late brother of Gen. Douglas MacArthur, 15th in 
2 CONC class) ; the widow of Capt. Roland I. Curtin (24th in class); Capt. Mason E. Mitchell of Arkansas (not an actual USNA graduate); and the wife of Commodore Bernard L. Austin (also not an actual USNA graduate), who was serving as ai
2 CONC d to Admiral Nimitz in Guam. (Mark was 19th in the USNA Class of 1896.)
2 CONT *1946, April 12: Mark St. Clair Ellis (age 73) and Rose (age 62) are both living in Annapolis, according to an item in the Northwest Arkansas Times. They drove Dr. Ruth Ellis Lesh, their niece, to Fayetteville, after she visited P
2 CONC hiladelphia and Annapolis.
2 CONT *1950, Mar. 2: Mark and Rose were listed as having been winter guests at the El Cordova in Coronado, California, according to the Eagle and Journal, the town's local paper.
2 CONT *1950, May 4: Mark and Rose return from Fayetteville, Arkansas, to Wardour, Maryland, according to the Annapolis Capital News.
2 CONT *1952, May 6: Mark St. Clair Ellis and Rose St. John Mildmay are listed as defendants in a suit announcement in the Northwest Arkansas Times of Fayetteville, brought on by a couple named DeJenrianis on April 14.
2 CONT *1952, May 27: Mark St. Clair Ellis dies at age 79. He is later buried at Arlington National Cemetery. (When Rose dies on June 23, 1973, in Fayetteville, she too will be buried there.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From NavSource Online: Service Ship Photo USS Hartford (IX-13) USS Hartford, Screw Sloop of War, 22 Nov. 1858 - 20 Nov. 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.navsource.org/archives/09/46/46013a.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The USS Hartford had a relationship with one of America’s most prolific artists and illustrators along with one of this countries beloved presidents, Franklin Roosevelt. In 1918 a young artist named Norman Rockwell arrived at the 
2 CONC Charleston Shipyard. Another twist of fate involving the Hartford put Mr. Rockwell at the shipyard. He was originally deployed from New York on his way to Ireland to paint insignias on aircraft. Before clearing the New York harbor
2 CONC  his ship was stopped by an American submarine. The sub’s Captain ordered Rockwell’s ship south to Charleston to avoid a German submarine which was lying off the coast. Upon Rockwell’s arrival in Charleston he was assigned to the 
2 CONC yard’s newspaper Afloat and Ashore as an artist. His tenure on the Hartford began with a chance encounter with the yard’s new Commanding Officer, '''Mark St. Clair Ellis''', who had his headquarters on the Hartford. In Mr. Rockwel
2 CONC l’s words “One afternoon O’Toole and I were in my studio discussing the situation (the new Commanding Officer) when the door was suddenly flung open, and a voice yelled “A-ten-shun!” and in walked the new commander in full-dress u
2 CONC niform. White jacked and trousers, gold braid and white gloves. A big handsome beefy looking fellow. His wife, a square mannish lady smartly dressed accompanied him. “Carry on, men carry on,” he said. I went back to work on the po
2 CONC rtrait I was painting and O’Toole fussed with a pile of old canvases in the corner. The new commander stood behind me looking at the portrait. After a minute I happened to glance around and saw that he was leaning on my palette ta
2 CONC ble, one of his white gloved hands squished down in the gobs and smears of oil paint. Oh, Lord, I thought , Siberia, here I come. “Ah, Commander, Sir, ”I said, “your hand, sir. Paint, sir.” He lifted his hand and looked at the glo
2 CONC ve sticky with paint of many colors. I composed a brave farewell letter to my friends at home. But then he laughed and , stripping off his glove, said, “A souvenir,” and handed it to me. He chatted a bit, asking about my work and 
2 CONC how I liked the Navy, and left the room.” At this time Rockwell was stilled employed by the Saturday Evening Post producing his famous front cover illustrations. He was allowed to continue his employment as long as the subject mat
2 CONC ter was about the U.S. Navy. The next day, Rockwell was transferred to Commander Ellis’s staff and moved his studio aboard the Hartford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not long afterwards the war ended. On 12 November 1918 Rockwell was discharged and his brief career in the Navy ended. The following is a description of the interior of the Hartford by Norman Rockwell. “In the center of the ship a
2 CONC  grand, red carpeted staircase swept down to a huge ballroom whose walls were decorated with ornate, hand carved scrollwork. The staterooms, which were the officer’s quarters, were lavishly appointed and hung with all manner of ri
2 CONC ch velvets and tapestries. Down all the carpeted hallways ran handrails of gleaming brass. The kitchen was staffed by a horde of cooks, meat cooks, pastry cooks, vegetable cooks, salad cooks, sauce cooks, etc. A marine band, scarl
2 CONC et jackets, blue trousers, white belts, and all, was kept always at the readiness and marines in full dress uniforms, dark blue coats, light blue trousers guarded the gangplanks.
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT August 6, 1936 - Reno Gazette-Journal from Reno, Nevada • Page 10
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/147963688/
2 CONT
2 CONT Divorced husband seeks share in estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Denver, Aug. 6 (UP) -  Commander Mark St. Clair Ellis, U. S. N., divorced husband of Mrs. Lena Allen Stoiber, wealthy Colorado woman who died in Italy last year, laid claim in county court here today to $138,600 and a $6000 life a
2 CONC nnuity from estate. Commander Ellis, now a resident of New York, filed five separate claims against Mrs. Stoiber's estate. He asks $100,000 plus interest from March 27, 1935, the date of Mrs. Stoiber's death, for the surrender at 
2 CONC her request of a note dated August 16, 1918. He surrendered the note, he said, on her promise to pay him that sum upon her death. He also seeks $12,000 with interest from the date of her death, as a sum on a bank draft of May 22, 
2 CONC 1918. He… Stoiber promised to return the sum in her will if it had not been taken care of before her death. Commander Ellis and Mrs. Ellis were divorced in Switzerland on March 15, 1932. At that time she was given permission to re
2 CONC sume the name of her first husband, Edward G. Stoiber, Colorado silver mining king who died in Paris in 1904. The major portion of Mrs. Stoiber's estate was left to a brother of her first husband, Louis Stoiber of Newark, N. J., a
2 CONC nd a sister, Mrs. Esther A. Jobs of Portland, Ore., who since has died. More than $150,000 was willed to friends. Commander Ellis was bequeathed the wardroom furniture of his battleship station while he was on active duty and $500
2 CONC 0. Two notes of $5000 each were cancelled. The will was permitted to probate May 11.
