1 NAME Samuel /Buckwalter/
2 GIVN Samuel
2 SURN Buckwalter
2 _MARNM Buckwalter
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Morris Cemetery, Phoenixville, Chester County, Pennsylvania, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 MAY 1801
2 PLAC East Nantmeal, Chester County, Pennsylvania, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 FEB 1869
2 PLAC Charlestown, Chester County, Pennsylvania, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} History of Chester County, Pennsylvania: With Genealogical and Biographical; By John Smith Futhey, Gilbert Cope 1881. Page 486
2 CONT ===============================================================
2 CONT "....Of these children, Samuel, the eldest, was born May 6, 1801, in East Nantmeal township, and in 1817-18 went to Charlestown township to live with his grandfather, Johannes (John), on the farm on which John Henry Buckwalter now
2 CONC  resides. In 1832 he was married by Rev. Jacob Wampole to Mary, daughter of Daniel and Margaret High, of Schuylkill township, by whom he had seven children, - Charles C.; John Henry; Samuel R.; Elizabeth, married to Enoch J. Davis
2 CONC ; and Helen Caroline, all living; and two deceased, Daniel and Margaret, who died young. Samuel Buckwalter died Feb. 26, 1869, and his wife, Mary (High), Jan. 4, 1850. He was a second time married, in 1855, to Ann Pennypacker, wid
2 CONC ow of James Pennypacker. He was one of the most systematic farmers in the county, and paid special attention to feeding good stock, in which he greatly excelled, and his farm and stock were kept always in good condition he was, wi
2 CONC th his family, a member of the Mennonite Church. He served as a school director and frequently in other township offices. He was an Old-Line Whig in politics and later a pronounced Republican. He was at the tie of his death presid
2 CONC ent of the Phoenixville National Bank, in which he was a very large stockholder. Of his children, Charles C. lives in the neighborhood of the old homestead farm, on which resides his brother, John Henry, the popular and efficient 
2 CONC county treasurer, elected in 1878 for a term of three years, and the remaining brother, Samuel K., is in active business at Phoenixville. He was known as a successful business man and a model farmer. Respected in the community for
2 CONC  purity of character, he was a worthy representative of his well-known family in the county"
2 CONT
2 CONT Thanks to find-a-graver Graves for providing the above bio.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/67429954/samuel-buckwalter Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 20 2
2 CONC 019, 20:22:37 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=76969734
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1713 the Manovon tract, at what is now Phoenizville, was patented to David Lloyd. The earliest settler upon it was Francis Buckwalter, to whom Lloyd sold 650 acres in 1720, for £195. Buckwalter, a Protestant refugee from German
2 CONC y, as subjected when in the Fatherland to many persecutions because of his faith, and it was a matter of family history that he was compelled to read his Bible by stealth, concealed in a cow trough. He finally concluded to flee, a
2 CONC nd after leaving his home was pursued for 3 days by his vindictive Catholic brothers, who were determined upon his destruction. His children were Joseph, Jacob, Johannes, Mary and Yost, and from him are descended all of the Buckwa
2 CONC lter family in this county. Of these, Johannes' son John, b Sep[t 14, 1777 married July 27, 1800, Mary Bechtel, b Jan 2, 1775, and their children were Samuel, John, David,
2 CONT Henry, James, and Elizabeth, who married James Wynn. Of these children, Samuel the eldest was born May 5, 1801, in East Nantmeal tpw, and in 1817-18 went to Charlestown twp to live with his grandfather, Johannes (John), on the far
2 CONC m on which John Henry Buckwalter now resides. In 1832 he was married by Rev Jacob Wampole to Mary, dau of Daniel and Margaret High, of Schuylkill twp, by whom he had 7 children - Charles C; John Henry; Samuel R; Elizabeth, married
2 CONC  to Enoch J Davis; and Helen Caroline, all living; and 2 deceased, David and Margaret, who died young. Samuel Buckwalter died Feb 26, 1869, and his wife, Mary High, Jan 4, 1850. He was a second time married, in 1855, to Ann Pennyp
2 CONC acker, widow of James Pennypacker. He was one of the most systematic farmers in the county and was, with his family, a member of the Mennonite Church.
