1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Josiah Closson was a soldier in King Phillip's War (as was his father-in- law) and credited on 24 June 1676 under Captain Joseph Syll (spelling que stionable). He was also a garrison of Chelmsford. He was given an 
2 CONC assignm ent of wages on 24 August 1676 to Woburn Town. He returned to Woburn an d was a taxpayer there after the War.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1681-2, Josiah Closson moved from Marshfield to Little Compton, R.I. , then an outpost of Plymouth Colony, Mass. In 1685 he was constable of L ittle Compton. On each of the following dates he bought land in Little Co mpton: 23 
2 CONC Nov 1694, 23 Dec 1694, and on 21 Dec 1698.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah Closson died intestate in 1698/9. The probate court of Bristol Co unty appointed his widow, Mary, administratrix.
2 CONT
2 CONT Josiah Closson and Mary Williamson had eight children. Their fourth daug hter, Mary, born jan 05, 1687, in Little Compton married John Bull.
2 CONT
2 CONT source: "Little Compton Families" by Benjamin Franklin Wilbour. publishe d by Little Compton Historical Society, Little Compton, R.I. (1967)
2 CONT
2 CONT Doherty:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The emigrant ancestor of the Closson family was Josiah Closson who wa s born ca. 1655 and was of Marshfield, MA and Little compton, RI." cite s an undated and authorless "Closson Genealogy"
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oshelp.com/houghtonfamily/nancy_gilchrist%20linage.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The first record of Josiah Closson is at Woburn, MA., in 1776 when he was a soldier in King Phillips war for that town. It is speculated that perhaps he met Captain Timothy Williamson during the war and moved to Marshfield, MA. Th
2 CONC is is where he met and married Mary Williamson, daughter of Timothy Howland and Mary Howland. Two of his eight children were born here.  About 1682 he followed Col. Benjamin Church, the leader of the Plymouth forces in King Philip
2 CONC s war, who had started a settlement at Little Crompton, RI. It is here that the other six children were born. Josiah died at the age of 44. He had amassed 37 acres in that part of  Little Crompton called Coxet, near the RI and MA 
2 CONC dorder.
2 CONT --------------------
