1 CONT 22 February 1616 - 17 January 1679, noting that alternative dates of 
1 CONT birth 28 December 1615 and 28 February 1616 and death of 16 January 
1 CONT 1679 are also found in other references. Five children Herman 
1 CONT Isaacs, Derick Isaacs, Abraham Isaacs, Margaretha (Margrit) and 
1 CONT Jacob, noting that other sources add Adolphus and Vonder. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Dora Smith, Op Den Graeff Ancestry, 
1 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Acres/5127/Webster/herman.html, 17 
1 CONT July 2000. Notes that some sources state he (like his father) had 
1 CONT eighteen children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth also given as 28 February 1615/1616.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a convert to the Quaker faith.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wilhelm Niepoth, The Ancestry of the Thirteen Krefeld Emigrants of 
1 CONT 1683. Isaak op den Graeff, 28 February 1616 - 17 January 1669. 
1 CONT Married Grietgen Peters.
0 @N6000000007283508363@ NOTE
1 CONT Judy Goldbaum, 17 May 2000.
1 CONT
1 CONT Donna N. Basinger, Clymer Connections, RootsWeb's WorldConnect
1 CONT Project, 22 May 2000.  Derick op den Graf.
1 CONT
1 CONT June (Shaull) Lutz 1988, History of the Op den Graef/Updegraff
1 CONT Family, pp. U-6 and U-394.  Derick, Derek, or Dirck.  He and his wife
1 CONT came to Pennsylvania in 1683.  Apparently there were no children.  He
1 CONT was a linenmaker.  One of the first burgesses of Germantown.  1692
1 CONT served as one of the town's six committeemen.  1693 served as
1 CONT bailiff.  1694 appointed to work to keep the town's exemption from
1 CONT taxation.  Will dated 10 November 1688 left all his property to wife
1 CONT Nilcken.  Approved 24 May 1697 in Administration Book A, p. 237.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dora Smith, Op Den Graeff Ancestry,
1 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Acres/5127/Webster/herman.html, 17
1 CONT July 2000.  No children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Paul Redden, Descendants of Herman op den Graef.  Immigrant 6 October
1 CONT 1683.  Signatory to a 1688 antislavery petition.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wilhelm Niepoth, The Ancestry of the Thirteen Krefeld Emigrants of
1 CONT 1683.  Dirk Isaaks or Derick Isacks  op dem Graeff arrived
1 CONT Pennsylvania 6 October 1683.  Also Derijck Isacks in his marriage
1 CONT record.
1 CONT
1 CONT The only one of the op den Graeff brothers to have retained his
1 CONT father's name as part of his surname.
0 @N6000000007283499020@ NOTE
1 CONT Judy Goldbaum, 17 May 2000. Herman Isaacs Updengraeff.
1 CONT
1 CONT June (Shaull) Lutz 1988, History of the Op den Graef/Updegraff 
1 CONT Family, pp. U-394/395. Married sisters Lisbet/Liesbet Isaacs van 
1 CONT Bebber and Debra/Debora van Bebber. He came to Germantown in 1683 as 
1 CONT one of the 13 original founders known as "the Krefelders." Before 
1 CONT leaving Germany Herman and his brothers Derick and Abraham purchased 
1 CONT 2000 acres of land in Pennsylvania from Jacob Telner. They (with 
1 CONT their mother and sister Margaret) sailed on the Concord from Krefeld, 
1 CONT leaving Gravesend, England, 24 July 1683 and arriving in Philadelphia 
1 CONT on 6 October 1683. (A cousin Willem Strepers of Krefeld was also on 
1 CONT board this ship.) One of the 11 charter members of the Germantown 
1 CONT Corporation. Served as Town President (he signed the charter 
1 CONT incorporating the Borough of Germantown as president) and as one of 
1 CONT the first burgesses. One of the authors of the "Remonstrance." 
1 CONT Operated a linen industry and farmed his own land, as well as acted 
1 CONT as agent for Jacob Telner and Dirck Sipman, other large land owners.
1 CONT 1690: sold land in Germantown to Garrett Hendricks DeWees.
1 CONT 1692: assessed 80 pounds and taxed 6 shillings by the General 
1 CONT Assembly, in the first taxation law. He subsequently signed 
1 CONT petitions 10 March 1692 and 15 May 1693 to defeat the tax bills.
1 CONT 2 December 1699: 116 acres of land were granted to Heivert 
1 CONT Paupen in Crefelt, Germany, by Herman Updegraph of Germantown, 
1 CONT weaver.
1 CONT Circa 29 September 1701, he moved to Mispillion Head, Kent 
1 CONT County, the Territories (now Delaware).
1 CONT Herman joined the Society of Friends after his settlement in 
1 CONT Pennsylvania. 
1 CONT Will was written 13 September 1708 (3?) and probated 18 December 
1 CONT 1708. Heir and executor wife Deborah. Children unnamed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Descendants of Herman Op-Den-Graeff of Germany, b. 1585, 
1 CONT http://members.aol.com/swinfield9/opdengraeff.htm, 18 June 2000. 
1 CONT Died 7 November 1708 in New Kent County, Delaware.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dora Smith, 3 March 1999, message #187 of the Archive for The 
1 CONT Original 13 Germantown Settlers. The Quaker settlement in 
1 CONT Pennsylvania was harmonious until 1692 until there occurred a 
1 CONT religious split. Herman and his brother Abraham joined with Keith, 
1 CONT but brother Dirck remained with the established Quakers. Dirck and 
1 CONT Abraham were particularly strong in their opposition to the other's 
1 CONT views.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles P. Keith 1917, Chronicles of Pennsylvania from the English 
1 CONT Revolution to the Peace of Aix-La-Chapelle 1688-1748, Chapter VIII, 
1 CONT Religious Dissension. The Society of Friends had religious practices 
1 CONT which set them apart from other religious creeds and marked them as 
1 CONT Quakers, but their tenets were informal with no insistence upon a 
1 CONT particular set of beliefs or dogma; rather each individual was to be 
1 CONT guided by his or her own inner faith. The failure to define and 
1 CONT adhere to the basic fundamentals of Christian theology engendered 
1 CONT opposition from Prebyterians, Episcopalians, and Congregationalists. 
1 CONT George Keith was a prominent Quaker preacher who believed that 
1 CONT Quakers needed a basic statement of faith; in 1691 he wrote a 
1 CONT declaration that the Friends accept basic and orthodox Christian 
1 CONT doctrines. Essentially Keith was undertaking a crusade to direct the 
1 CONT doctrine of the Society of Friends in Pennsylvania. Keith's 
1 CONT supporters, the "Christian Quakers" or "Separatists" or "Keithians," 
1 CONT maintained that their opponents were not true believers in Christ 
1 CONT Jesus, were not Christians, and thus could not be joined with in 
1 CONT worship; the Keithians instituted separate meetings. At the 
1 CONT Philadelphia Quarterly Meeting of the Society of Friends in 1692 
1 CONT Keith was condemned. Ultimately Keith left the Society of Friends 
1 CONT and joined the Church of England. Other Separatists joined the 
1 CONT Baptists or the Seventh Day Baptists. In due course the doctrines of 
1 CONT the remaining Quakers accepted Christian orthodoxy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pennsylvania State Library, Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. Last will and 
1 CONT testament of Herman Updegraff dated 13th day of the 9th month 1708. 
1 CONT Of Kent County upon Delaware Bay. Wife Deborah Updegrove to be 
1 CONT executrix. She to receive the estate; each child to receive 12 pence 
1 CONT in silver.
1 CONT
1 CONT Paul Redden, Descendants of herman op den Graef. There were also 
1 CONT children John op den Graeff; Joseph op den Graeff; and William op den 
1 CONT Graeff.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robin Boyer Kornides, Descendants of Herman OpdenGraff. Died 7 
1 CONT November 1708.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wilhelm Niepoth, The Ancestry of the Thirteen Krefeld Emigrants of 
1 CONT 1683. Hermann Isaaks op dem Graeff arrived Pennsylvania 6 October 
1 CONT 1863.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guidebook to Historical Germantown, 1902 Edition. First settlers 
1 CONT came from the lower Rhine country, near Holland. Thirteen families 
1 CONT (33 persons) left Crefeld in 1683 for London, where they embarked on 
1 CONT the ship Concord. Leaving London on 24 July, they arrived in 
1 CONT Philadelphia 6 October. On 23 October, thirteen settlers selected 
1 CONT their land by lot in the newly laid out Germantown Township; they 
1 CONT included Abraham op den Graeff, Herman op den Graeff, and Dirck op 
1 CONT den Graeff.
