0 @N6000000001985881591@ NOTE Enlisted in American army in Fredericksburg in 1775, in a company of riflemen commanded by Capt. David Morgan.  Served as a private during the whole Revolutionary War, was at Valley Forge and Brandywine, in campaigns against Cusbe
1 CONC e, Cowpens, and surrender of Burgoyne.  Also fought against Western Indians with McAntosh.  Received a pension by Act of Congress in 1818 (KY Society of Sons of American Revolution, p. 117; see also DAR records).
0 @N6000000003043343438@ NOTE Long Family and allied lines (long/1) has a much more exciting ancestry for him:
1 CONT John William of Cleves 1562-1609 < William V of Cleves 1516-1592 < John III of Cleves 1490-1539
1 CONT  < John II of Cleves 1458-1521 < John I of Cleves 1419-1481< Adolphus IV De La Marck 1371-1448,
1 CONT  with interesting names and parentage for some of the wives
0 @N6000000007283508399@ NOTE
1 CONT WARNING -- THE PARENTS OF HERMAN OP DEN GRAEFF AS GIVEN HERE ARE
1 CONT UNPROVEN AND ARE BASED SOLELY UPON THE ARGUMENTS PRESENTED BY G.W.
1 CONT MILLER.  THE VALIDITY OF HIS ARGUMENTS AND CONCLUSIONS HAVE YET TO BE
1 CONT PROVEN.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judy Goldbaum, 17 May 2000.  Born Muir, Germany.  Nineteen children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Donna N. Basinger, Clymer Connections, RootsWeb's WorldConnect
1 CONT Project, 22 May 2000.  Herman Isaacs op den Graf.  Born Aldekirk,
1 CONT Rheinland, Germany.  Marriage in Aldekirk.  Signatory of the 1632
1 CONT Dordrecht Confession of Faith (Mennonite Articles of Faith) as one of
1 CONT the two delegates from Krefeld.  Mennonite Bishop in Krefeld.
1 CONT Occupation: cloth and linen dealer.
1 CONT
1 CONT June (Shaull) Lutz 1988, History of the Op den Graef/Updegraff
1 CONT Family, p. U-2.  Married (6) August 1605 at Krefeld, Germany.  Linen
1 CONT weaver and merchant.  He was born at Aldekirk/Allekirche some 12
1 CONT miles from Krefeld.  He was a burher of Kempen in 1605 where he
1 CONT married.  He moved to Krefeld by about 1608.  He is the first
1 CONT Mennonite preacher known by name.  Eighteen children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Father often given (WITHOUT PROOF) as Abraham op den Graf, with
1 CONT family lineages as follows:
1 CONT 1)  FREDERIKUS DE GROBEN, m. 1359 CATHARINE DE SOMMEROCK
1 CONT 2)  LEONARDUS DE GROBEN, m. 1441 MARIA DE NORTHEIN
1 CONT 3)  Ernetus DE GROBEN (d. 1510), m. 1508 Margarethe DE RITSCHES
1 CONT
1 CONT 3)  Virgilina DE GROBEN (1467 - 1507)
1 CONT 3)  WOLFGANGUS DE GROBEN
1 CONT 4)  Pieter OP DEN GRAEFF (aft 1483 - ), m. Grietz Pietrazs
1 CONT BERENTS
1 CONT 4)  ABRAHAM OP DEN GRAF (1490 - 1561 Dusseldorf, Germany)
1 CONT
1 CONT Cf. Donna N. Basinger, Clymer Connections, RootsWeb's WorldConnect
1 CONT Project, 22 May 2000.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cf. June (Shaull) Lutz 1988, History of the Op den Graef/Updegraff
1 CONT Family, pp. U-1/U-2.  From a 17th century document.
1 CONT Unproven as father of Herman op den Graeff.  Abraham was a
1 CONT Protestant reformer (1561) of Zwammerdam, Holland, whose lands were
1 CONT seized by the Catholic church.  He and his family escaped to Antwerp,
1 CONT but persecution of Protestants drove them to Germany.  After his
1 CONT death his widow moved to Krefeld.
1 CONT Family name apparently was originally VonGrober.  The VonGrobers
1 CONT or DeGraeffs/Op den Graeffs were part of the royal family of Holland
1 CONT (but this is not verified).
1 CONT
1 CONT http://sml.simplenet.com/reunion/haller/ps23/ps23_213.htm.  Hillekin
1 CONT op den Graeff.  Also gives a child 1606-1606 and Sophia (Teiken) 1628
1 CONT - ?.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two painted glass windows, circa 1630, from the home of Herman op den
1 CONT Graeff are preserved in the Kaiser Wilhelm Museum in Krefeld.
1 CONT
1 CONT G. W. Miller 1991, "Reconstructing the Op Den Graff windows of 1630
1 CONT A.D. to fit the Lohengrin Genealogy of the House of Cleves," Krefeld
1 CONT Immigrants and Their Descendants, vol. 8, #1, pp. 9-28.
1 CONT Interpretation of heraldic symbols on the op den Graff windows
1 CONT implies that Herman op den Graeff was the son of John William de la
1 CONT Marck, Count of Altena, and a woman not of the nobility.
1 CONT 1605, registered as Hermann zu Graff in his marriage to Grietje
1 CONT Pletjes.  He became a linen merchant and a leader of the Mennonite
1 CONT Community in Krefeld, under the jurisdiction of the Protestant House
1 CONT of Orange.
1 CONT 1616, he presented a sum of money to the Monsatery of Meer (Moers) so
1 CONT that four nuns (the sister von Brackel and sister Gudula von Arfft
1 CONT who were living in his home in Krefeld) could live in the Monastery
1 CONT of the Franciscan Nuns in Krefeld.
1 CONT 1637, termed Der Hiesigen Mennisten Herrn Bischoff (Local Mennonite
1 CONT Lord Bishop).
1 CONT
1 CONT G. W. Miller 1996, "The Transfiguration of the Windows," Krefeld
1 CONT Immigrants and Their Descendants, vol. 13, #2, pp. 51-67.  A second
1 CONT discussion of the thesis presented in 1991.  While these discussions
1 CONT are suggestive (and provisionally accepted here), there is no real
1 CONT proof that identification of Herman's parents is correct.  It is not
1 CONT apparent that the two stained glass windows which form a major part
1 CONT of the argument have actually been seen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dora Smith, Doors (Thiessen, Tyson) Family of Krefeld,
1 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Acres/5127/Webster/doors.html, 17
1 CONT July 2000.  Also Dora Smith, Op Den Graeff Ancestry,
1 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Acres/5127/Webster/herman.html, 17
1 CONT July 2000.
1 CONT Much of the genealogy of the op den Graeffs stems from the
1 CONT Scheuten Manuscripts, a private Scheuten family history  allegedly
1 CONT written by Adam Scheuten (1639-1668) or his son [sic] Abraham
1 CONT Scheuten (1707-1789), though the actual authorship appears unknown.
1 CONT This history exists in several versions without any documentation or
1 CONT sources named to support the genealogy.  It suggests that Herman's
1 CONT grandmother was a Habsburg princess, his father was an Abraham Graeff
1 CONT of Kempden, his mother a Catholic nun, and his wife was a Plantagenet
1 CONT member of the English Norman royal family.  There is the suspicion
1 CONT that this manuscript was contrived to invent a royal ancestry for the
1 CONT op den Graeff family.
1 CONT Eighteen children are suggested, but various sources differ.
1 CONT Much of this genealogy stems from the undocumented Scheuten
1 CONT Manuscripts in their several forms.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dora Smith, Archive for The Original Germantown Settlers, message
1 CONT #538, 4 June 1999.  Herman op den Graeff was an extreme mystic -- the
1 CONT supposed genealogical material of the Krefeld stained glass windows
1 CONT is actually mystical religious symbolism borrowed from several faiths
1 CONT and sects.  The links of the op den Graeff family to other Krefeld
1 CONT families with noble linkages has not been established and traces back
1 CONT to one of the versions of the Scheuten Manuscript.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wilhelm Niepoth, The Ancestry of the Thirteen Krefeld Emigrants of
1 CONT 1683.  Townsman in Kemper 1605.  To Krefeld about 1609 where he died
1 CONT 27 November 1642.
